[Event "Chapter 1: White's Main Setup / Study 1: The 2 Knights Introduction / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/f73efe60-d94d-4682-9a60-0ac5644b41f9/studies/96ebcba0-d9ce-4d6c-8c6e-391ebbd48173/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of this course! In this study we will demonstrate our e6/b6/Bb7 setup, and we'll see what to do after White plays both their pawns to e4/d4 and their knights to c3 and f3. Against 1.e4, we play 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {A second pawn joins the center for White, and the bishop's diagonal is opened. We will reply with 2...b6, fianchettoing our bishop, as will be our intention throughout this course.} 2...b6 3.Nf3 {White develops, supporting the center. We will counter by putting pressure on the e4-pawn.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White develops to c3, supporting their center! The two knights setup is complete! We will respond by developing our dark-squared bishop, pinning the c3-knight. This will leave White with four options which we will examine in this study.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {This option, defending the pawn, will also be seen later on. But in here we can demonstrate motifs using a lesser-known line White might play. We will reply by developing our knight, putting further pressure on the center.} 5...Nf6 6.Nd2 {One option is for White to retreat, defending e4 and breaking the pin. The knight on f3 supported the d-pawn, but none of our pieces are currently attacking d4. Therefore, we will target the d4-pawn right away.} 6...c5 7.dxc5 {Naturally, White can simply take. We will reply, not by recapturing, but with further development. Bringing our knight to c6 will also discourage e5 by White.} 7...Nc6 8.cxb6 {If they take another pawn, we can simply recapture and open up our rook.} 8...axb6 9.O-O {And when White castles, we will capture on c3, shattering their queenside pawns.} 9...Bxc3 10.bxc3 {Of course, White recaptures.} 10...Qc7 {We develop our queen. We have open lines, and the isolated pawns on the a and c-files will come under pressure!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White's Main Setup / Study 1: The 2 Knights Introduction / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/f73efe60-d94d-4682-9a60-0ac5644b41f9/studies/96ebcba0-d9ce-4d6c-8c6e-391ebbd48173/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of this course! In this study we will demonstrate our e6/b6/Bb7 setup, and we'll see what to do after White plays both their pawns to e4/d4 and their knights to c3 and f3. Against 1.e4, we play 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {A second pawn joins the center for White, and the bishop's diagonal is opened. We will reply with 2...b6, fianchettoing our bishop, as will be our intention throughout this course.} 2...b6 3.Nf3 {White develops, supporting the center. We will counter by putting pressure on the e4-pawn.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White develops to c3, supporting their center! The two knights setup is complete! We will respond by developing our dark-squared bishop, pinning the c3-knight. This will leave White with four options which we will examine in this study.} 4...Bb4 5.d5 {White can try pushing the other central pawn. This will turn out to be premature, as we can bring further pressure to bear on the center by developing our king's knight.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {White pins our knight, trying to neutralize our pressure. We will reply by pushing the h-pawn, and putting a question to the bishop.} 6...h6 7.Bxf6 {White captures on f6.} 7...Qxf6 {But when we recapture, White is in trouble because of the lack of support for their overextended central pawns and our pressure on c3.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White's Main Setup / Study 1: The 2 Knights Introduction / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/f73efe60-d94d-4682-9a60-0ac5644b41f9/studies/96ebcba0-d9ce-4d6c-8c6e-391ebbd48173/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of this course! In this study we will demonstrate our e6/b6/Bb7 setup, and we'll see what to do after White plays both their pawns to e4/d4 and their knights to c3 and f3. Against 1.e4, we play 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {A second pawn joins the center for White, and the bishop's diagonal is opened. We will reply with 2...b6, fianchettoing our bishop, as will be our intention throughout this course.} 2...b6 3.Nf3 {White develops, supporting the center. We will counter by putting pressure on the e4-pawn.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White develops to c3, supporting their center! The two knights setup is complete! We will respond by developing our dark-squared bishop, pinning the c3-knight. This will leave White with four options which we will examine in this study.} 4...Bb4 5.e5 {White can opt to gain space.} 5...d6 {This move allows us to strike immediately at the center with 5...d6, and we will have easy development of our knights along our second rank.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White's Main Setup / Study 1: The 2 Knights Introduction / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/f73efe60-d94d-4682-9a60-0ac5644b41f9/studies/96ebcba0-d9ce-4d6c-8c6e-391ebbd48173/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of this course! In this study we will demonstrate our e6/b6/Bb7 setup, and we'll see what to do after White plays both their pawns to e4/d4 and their knights to c3 and f3. Against 1.e4, we play 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {A second pawn joins the center for White, and the bishop's diagonal is opened. We will reply with 2...b6, fianchettoing our bishop, as will be our intention throughout this course.} 2...b6 3.Nf3 {White develops, supporting the center. We will counter by putting pressure on the e4-pawn.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White develops to c3, supporting their center! The two knights setup is complete! We will respond by developing our dark-squared bishop, pinning the c3-knight. This will leave White with four options which we will examine in this study.} 4...Bb4 5.Qd3 {White defends e4 with the queen. We will develop our king's knight on e7, where it is safe from an e5 pawn thrust, and where it also supports a ...d5 pawn push.} 5...Ne7 6.Be2 {White develops their bishop and gets ready to castle.} 6...d5 {We can strike in the center! In the following studies, we'll analyze a bit deeper into the middle game!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White's Main Setup / Study 1: The 2 Knights Introduction / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/f73efe60-d94d-4682-9a60-0ac5644b41f9/studies/96ebcba0-d9ce-4d6c-8c6e-391ebbd48173/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of this course! In this study we will demonstrate our e6/b6/Bb7 setup, and we'll see what to do after White plays both their pawns to e4/d4 and their knights to c3 and f3. Against 1.e4, we play 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {A second pawn joins the center for White, and the bishop's diagonal is opened. We will reply with 2...b6, fianchettoing our bishop, as will be our intention throughout this course.} 2...b6 3.Nf3 {White develops, supporting the center. We will counter by putting pressure on the e4-pawn.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White develops to c3, supporting their center! The two knights setup is complete! We will respond by developing our dark-squared bishop, pinning the c3-knight. This will leave White with four options which we will examine in this study.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {This option, defending the pawn, will also be seen later on. But in here we can demonstrate motifs using a lesser-known line White might play. We will reply by developing our knight, putting further pressure on the center.} 5...Nf6 6.Nd2 {One option is for White to retreat, defending e4 and breaking the pin. The knight on f3 supported the d-pawn, but none of our pieces are currently attacking d4. Therefore, we will target the d4-pawn right away.} 6...c5 7.a3 {White could also attack our bishop, after all our c5 pawn push has taken away any retreat squares. We will capture on d4, with a counterattack on the c3-knight.} 7...cxd4 8.axb4 {White takes our bishop, so we will capture their knight.} 8...dxc3 9.bxc3 {White recaptures the pawn on c3. The exchanges have resulted in material parity.} 9...Qc7 {Our queen comes out to c7, putting pressure on c3.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White's Main Setup / Study 2: White Plays 6. e5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/f73efe60-d94d-4682-9a60-0ac5644b41f9/studies/a8e6331c-d956-4c38-9674-dfc33e331fcf/lines"]

1.e4 {This second study will look at what happens after 5.Bd3 to defend the e4 pawn. In particular, we will look at the continuation 5...Nf6, adding another attacker, and in response the pawn advance 6.e5. White starts by establishing a central presence with e4. As before, we will reply with ...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {White places a second pawn in the center, and we will reply with 2...b6 to fianchetto our bishop.} 2...b6 3.Nf3 {The knight develops, defending d4. We play our traditional fianchetto.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White develops with Nc3, defending e4. We will respond with ...Bb4, pinning the knight.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {This moves adds another defender to e4, as the knight on c3 is pinned. We will add another piece placing pressure on e4.} 5...Nf6 6.e5 {The e-pawn attacks the knight, and removes itself from danger. We will utilize the pin on the c3-knight to bring our knight to an aggressive post on e4.} 6...Ne4 7.Bd2 {This move breaks the pin and thus attacks our e4-knight! We will reply by capturing on d2, gaining the bishop pair.} 7...Nxd2 8.Qxd2 {White recaptures with the queen and develops her at the same time. We will take on f3, doubling White's kingside pawns and also removing a defender of the d4-pawn.} 8...Bxf3 9.gxf3 {Recapturing the bishop. We will continue to exchange minor pieces. Another decent option would be to finish our development with 9...Nc6.} 9...Bxc3 10.Qxc3 {Other players might choose to recapture with the queen. We will reply by developing our knight.} 10...Nc6 11.O-O-O {And when White castles...} 11...Ne7 {We'll bring our knight to e7 where it will help cover the d5 and f5 squares. If a rook comes to the g-file once we have castled kingside, g6 will be a good move to stop counterplay on the file. We may consider bringing a rook to the c-file in combination with a ...c5 pawn break in order to open up a line against the white king.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White's Main Setup / Study 2: White Plays 6. e5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/f73efe60-d94d-4682-9a60-0ac5644b41f9/studies/a8e6331c-d956-4c38-9674-dfc33e331fcf/lines"]

1.e4 {This second study will look at what happens after 5.Bd3 to defend the e4 pawn. In particular, we will look at the continuation 5...Nf6, adding another attacker, and in response the pawn advance 6.e5. White starts by establishing a central presence with e4. As before, we will reply with ...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {White places a second pawn in the center, and we will reply with 2...b6 to fianchetto our bishop.} 2...b6 3.Nf3 {The knight develops, defending d4. We play our traditional fianchetto.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White develops with Nc3, defending e4. We will respond with ...Bb4, pinning the knight.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {This moves adds another defender to e4, as the knight on c3 is pinned. We will add another piece placing pressure on e4.} 5...Nf6 6.e5 {The e-pawn attacks the knight, and removes itself from danger. We will utilize the pin on the c3-knight to bring our knight to an aggressive post on e4.} 6...Ne4 7.Bd2 {This move breaks the pin and thus attacks our e4-knight! We will reply by capturing on d2, gaining the bishop pair.} 7...Nxd2 8.Qxd2 {White recaptures with the queen and develops her at the same time. We will take on f3, doubling White's kingside pawns and also removing a defender of the d4-pawn.} 8...Bxf3 9.gxf3 {Recapturing the bishop. We will continue to exchange minor pieces. Another decent option would be to finish our development with 9...Nc6.} 9...Bxc3 10.bxc3 {White can recapture and get another pawn to support the center. A player might choose this option if they want more open lines.} 10...O-O {The king gets to safety. We should expect some sort of kingside attack from White, but the relative lack of minor pieces means White has fewer forces to attack us with. We can push ...f5 to block White's bishop, and potentially also g6 to blunt pressure on the g-file. If they decide to take it en passant, our queen will get nicely activated along the f-file and exert some pressure.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White's Main Setup / Study 2: White Plays 6. e5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/f73efe60-d94d-4682-9a60-0ac5644b41f9/studies/a8e6331c-d956-4c38-9674-dfc33e331fcf/lines"]

1.e4 {This second study will look at what happens after 5.Bd3 to defend the e4 pawn. In particular, we will look at the continuation 5...Nf6, adding another attacker, and in response the pawn advance 6.e5. White starts by establishing a central presence with e4. As before, we will reply with ...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {White places a second pawn in the center, and we will reply with 2...b6 to fianchetto our bishop.} 2...b6 3.Nf3 {The knight develops, defending d4. We play our traditional fianchetto.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White develops with Nc3, defending e4. We will respond with ...Bb4, pinning the knight.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {This moves adds another defender to e4, as the knight on c3 is pinned. We will add another piece placing pressure on e4.} 5...Nf6 6.e5 {The e-pawn attacks the knight, and removes itself from danger. We will utilize the pin on the c3-knight to bring our knight to an aggressive post on e4.} 6...Ne4 7.Bxe4 {White might opt to relieve some of the pressure by capturing our knight. We will continue normally by recapturing.} 7...Bxe4 8.O-O {White castles, and we need to be careful! The White knight is now unpinned, and it can take our bishop on e4 if we allow it. Therefore we will remove the danger by capturing on c3, damaging White's pawn structure.} 8...Bxc3 9.bxc3 {White recaptures the only way possible.} 9...O-O {We castle into to safety. Our e4-bishop is a strong piece, giving us good control over the light squares and long diagonal.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White's Main Setup / Study 3: White Plays 6. Qe2 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/f73efe60-d94d-4682-9a60-0ac5644b41f9/studies/a4a27de5-0b07-49b7-af24-f9cecd4ec42e/lines"]

1.e4 {This is the third and final study of this chapter. We'll go over the variations that may arise after 6. Qe2. Master them, and you'll master the system! Let's play 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {White pushes another central pawn. We will prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 2...b6 3.Nf3 {White develops a piece and supports d4. We will fianchetto our light-squared bishop, applying pressure to e4.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White defends e4. We will pin the knight, thus placing e4 under attack once more.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {White defends e4. We will develop our knight, adding another attacker on e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Qe2 {Qe2 adds another defender for White, and we will continue to add pressure, while establishing one of our pawns in the center!} 6...d5 7.e5 {This is a very natural response. White thinks: if the pressure is too much on e4 to handle, just push the pawn to e5! There's less pressure there. We will respond by bringing our attacked knight into the outpost on e4.} 7...Ne4 8.O-O {White gets the king to safety. Additionally, they unpin the c3 knight! We will take on c3, and win a pawn.} 8...Nxc3 9.bxc3 {White recaptures, as they should. We will win a pawn with our bishop, and attack the a1-rook.} 9...Bxc3 10.Rb1 {White's rook gets out of danger. Our bishop is a little short of safe squares. In the meantime, we will continue by developing our knight and putting pressure on d4.} 10...Nc6 11.Qe3 {White defends d4. We will use the freshly developed knight on c6 to support the escape of our bishop via b4.} 11...Bb4 12.Qf4 {The queen gives the dark-squared bishop a square on e3. Additionally, she stares down at f7. We need to treat the active white pieces with respect, and accordingly, we play ...h6, denying the knight access to g5.} 12...h6 13.Bb5 {This move pins our knight which was defending our bishop! We can't leave it hanging, so we will retreat it to e7.} 13...Be7 14.Qg4 {The queen attacks the g7-pawn. If we were to castle, we would suddenly find ourselves walking into trouble after Bxh6, when the g-pawn is pinned. Therefore we push the g-pawn forward to g6.} 14...g6 15.c3 {White creates a strong central pawn chain. We will move our queen, preparing the king's escape to the queenside.} 15...Qd7 16.Ne1 {White prepares to expand with f4, and a later f5. We will continue by taking steps to break the awkward pin on the a4-e8 diagonal.} 16...a6 17.Ba4 {White chooses to maintain the pin, but we now have the potential option of ...b5. In the meantime, we will get the king into relative safety on the queenside.} 17...O-O-O 18.Be3 {White finally develops their queen's bishop.} 18...Qe8 {We continue to disentangle from the pin, with the idea of the queen heading to the kingside to assist with impending pawn pushes against the opposing king.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White's Main Setup / Study 3: White Plays 6. Qe2 / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/f73efe60-d94d-4682-9a60-0ac5644b41f9/studies/a4a27de5-0b07-49b7-af24-f9cecd4ec42e/lines"]

1.e4 {This is the third and final study of this chapter. We'll go over the variations that may arise after 6. Qe2. Master them, and you'll master the system! Let's play 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {White pushes another central pawn. We will prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 2...b6 3.Nf3 {White develops a piece and supports d4. We will fianchetto our light-squared bishop, applying pressure to e4.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White defends e4. We will pin the knight, thus placing e4 under attack once more.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {White defends e4. We will develop our knight, adding another attacker on e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Qe2 {Qe2 adds another defender for White, and we will continue to add pressure, while establishing one of our pawns in the center!} 6...d5 7.exd5 {A natural response. White gets rid of the pressure by trading off a pair of pawns. We will capture on d5 with the queen, exploiting the pin on the c3-knight in order to bring our queen to an active location.} 7...Qxd5 8.O-O {This is White's main move, and it breaks the pin, so we will now eliminate the knight attacking our queen.} 8...Bxc3 9.bxc3 {Recapturing. And now Black has the option of Nbd7 or Qh5. We prefer to develop another piece instead of moving the queen yet again unprovoked.} 9...Nbd7 10.c4 {This is a common advance by White, expanding in the center. We will keep the queen actively placed by moving it to h5.} 10...Qh5 11.Bf4 {Developing a piece and attacking c7. We will defend c7 with our rook, and prepare a future pawn push with ...c5.} 11...Rc8 12.a4 {Gaining queenside space, and with the idea of activating the a1-rook by opening the a-file. Launching an attack. We will prevent this by pushing ...a5 ourselves.} 12...a5 13.Rab1 {White redeploys the rook to an active post. We will simply castle, getting our king into safety.} 13...O-O 14.Rb5 {Attacking our queen. We will block with c5, simultaneously targeting the white center.} 14...c5 15.dxc5 {White resolves the tension.} 15...Rxc5 {And we recapture with our rook to avoid pinning our knight. White has the bishop pair, but our pawn structure is far better.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White's Main Setup / Study 3: White Plays 6. Qe2 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/f73efe60-d94d-4682-9a60-0ac5644b41f9/studies/a4a27de5-0b07-49b7-af24-f9cecd4ec42e/lines"]

1.e4 {This is the third and final study of this chapter. We'll go over the variations that may arise after 6. Qe2. Master them, and you'll master the system! Let's play 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {White pushes another central pawn. We will prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 2...b6 3.Nf3 {White develops a piece and supports d4. We will fianchetto our light-squared bishop, applying pressure to e4.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White defends e4. We will pin the knight, thus placing e4 under attack once more.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {White defends e4. We will develop our knight, adding another attacker on e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Qe2 {Qe2 adds another defender for White, and we will continue to add pressure, while establishing one of our pawns in the center!} 6...d5 7.e5 {This is a very natural response. White thinks: if the pressure is too much on e4 to handle, just push the pawn to e5! There's less pressure there. We will respond by bringing our attacked knight into the outpost on e4.} 7...Ne4 8.O-O {White gets the king to safety. Additionally, they unpin the c3 knight! We will take on c3, and win a pawn.} 8...Nxc3 9.bxc3 {White recaptures, as they should. We will win a pawn with our bishop, and attack the a1-rook.} 9...Bxc3 10.Rb1 {White's rook gets out of danger. Our bishop is a little short of safe squares. In the meantime, we will continue by developing our knight and putting pressure on d4.} 10...Nc6 11.Ba3 {White sacrifices the d4-pawn with the idea that taking away our kingside castling rights is sufficient compensation. Spoiler alert: it isn't.} 11...Nxd4 12.Nxd4 {White exchanges the knight, and we recapture.} 12...Bxd4 13.Qg4 {This move forks our bishop and g7 pawn. The only reasonable solution is to grab the pawn on e5!} 13...Bxe5 14.Rfe1 {White attacks our bishop with the rook, going for activity on the open lines. We will retreat the bishop, maintaining the connection to g7.} 14...Bf6 15.Rxe6+ {A rook sacrifice! White plays with the idea of catching the king in the center. We have no choice but to accept.} 15...fxe6 16.Qxe6+ {White recaptures with check! Our king has no squares, but we can block with the bishop.} 16...Be7 17.Re1 {White adds another attacker! We will block the attacking bishop with our c-pawn.} 17...c5 18.Bb2 {White redeploys the bishop on another diagonal, attacking the g7-pawn. We will defend it with our king, and this also breaks the pin on the e7-bishop.} 18...Kf8 19.Re5 {White keeps the attack going. Now Rf5+ is a big threat. We will preempt it by blocking the file with our bishop.} 19...Bf6 20.Rf5 {Rf5 pins our bishop to the king, and threatens to invade with Bxf6. We can still use our pawns to block the bishop.} 20...d4 {We could also have played ...Qe7. It is coming next move and we are safe, with an extra rook.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White's Main Setup / Study 3: White Plays 6. Qe2 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/f73efe60-d94d-4682-9a60-0ac5644b41f9/studies/a4a27de5-0b07-49b7-af24-f9cecd4ec42e/lines"]

1.e4 {This is the third and final study of this chapter. We'll go over the variations that may arise after 6. Qe2. Master them, and you'll master the system! Let's play 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {White pushes another central pawn. We will prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 2...b6 3.Nf3 {White develops a piece and supports d4. We will fianchetto our light-squared bishop, applying pressure to e4.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White defends e4. We will pin the knight, thus placing e4 under attack once more.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {White defends e4. We will develop our knight, adding another attacker on e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Qe2 {Qe2 adds another defender for White, and we will continue to add pressure, while establishing one of our pawns in the center!} 6...d5 7.e5 {This is a very natural response. White thinks: if the pressure is too much on e4 to handle, just push the pawn to e5! There's less pressure there. We will respond by bringing our attacked knight into the outpost on e4.} 7...Ne4 8.O-O {White gets the king to safety. Additionally, they unpin the c3 knight! We will take on c3, and win a pawn.} 8...Nxc3 9.bxc3 {White recaptures, as they should. We will win a pawn with our bishop, and attack the a1-rook.} 9...Bxc3 10.Rb1 {White's rook gets out of danger. Our bishop is a little short of safe squares. In the meantime, we will continue by developing our knight and putting pressure on d4.} 10...Nc6 11.Be3 {Alternatively, White can defend d4 with the bishop. We will reply by harassing the strong d3-bishop with our knight.} 11...Nb4 12.Bb5+ {White could check on b5, but this is met with a pawn interposition.} 12...c6 {We block the check, and pose a question to the bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White's Main Setup / Study 3: White Plays 6. Qe2 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/f73efe60-d94d-4682-9a60-0ac5644b41f9/studies/a4a27de5-0b07-49b7-af24-f9cecd4ec42e/lines"]

1.e4 {This is the third and final study of this chapter. We'll go over the variations that may arise after 6. Qe2. Master them, and you'll master the system! Let's play 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {White pushes another central pawn. We will prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 2...b6 3.Nf3 {White develops a piece and supports d4. We will fianchetto our light-squared bishop, applying pressure to e4.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White defends e4. We will pin the knight, thus placing e4 under attack once more.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {White defends e4. We will develop our knight, adding another attacker on e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Qe2 {Qe2 adds another defender for White, and we will continue to add pressure, while establishing one of our pawns in the center!} 6...d5 7.e5 {This is a very natural response. White thinks: if the pressure is too much on e4 to handle, just push the pawn to e5! There's less pressure there. We will respond by bringing our attacked knight into the outpost on e4.} 7...Ne4 8.O-O {White gets the king to safety. Additionally, they unpin the c3 knight! We will take on c3, and win a pawn.} 8...Nxc3 9.bxc3 {White recaptures, as they should. We will win a pawn with our bishop, and attack the a1-rook.} 9...Bxc3 10.Rb1 {White's rook gets out of danger. Our bishop is a little short of safe squares. In the meantime, we will continue by developing our knight and putting pressure on d4.} 10...Nc6 11.Be3 {Alternatively, White can defend d4 with the bishop. We will reply by harassing the strong d3-bishop with our knight.} 11...Nb4 12.Rb3 {White can attack our bishop, which has no escape squares, but we will capture on d3 in reply.} 12...Nxd3 13.Qxd3 {Recapturing with the queen, but our bishop can now sneak out to a5!} 13...Ba5 {We have managed to retain our extra pawn, and we have the bishop pair.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White's Main Setup / Study 3: White Plays 6. Qe2 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/f73efe60-d94d-4682-9a60-0ac5644b41f9/studies/a4a27de5-0b07-49b7-af24-f9cecd4ec42e/lines"]

1.e4 {This is the third and final study of this chapter. We'll go over the variations that may arise after 6. Qe2. Master them, and you'll master the system! Let's play 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {White pushes another central pawn. We will prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 2...b6 3.Nf3 {White develops a piece and supports d4. We will fianchetto our light-squared bishop, applying pressure to e4.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White defends e4. We will pin the knight, thus placing e4 under attack once more.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {White defends e4. We will develop our knight, adding another attacker on e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Qe2 {Qe2 adds another defender for White, and we will continue to add pressure, while establishing one of our pawns in the center!} 6...d5 7.e5 {This is a very natural response. White thinks: if the pressure is too much on e4 to handle, just push the pawn to e5! There's less pressure there. We will respond by bringing our attacked knight into the outpost on e4.} 7...Ne4 8.O-O {White gets the king to safety. Additionally, they unpin the c3 knight! We will take on c3, and win a pawn.} 8...Nxc3 9.bxc3 {White recaptures, as they should. We will win a pawn with our bishop, and attack the a1-rook.} 9...Bxc3 10.Rb1 {White's rook gets out of danger. Our bishop is a little short of safe squares. In the meantime, we will continue by developing our knight and putting pressure on d4.} 10...Nc6 11.Be3 {Alternatively, White can defend d4 with the bishop. We will reply by harassing the strong d3-bishop with our knight.} 11...Nb4 12.Rb3 {White can attack our bishop, which has no escape squares, but we will capture on d3 in reply.} 12...Nxd3 13.Rxc3 {If rook takes on d3 instead of the queen recapture, then our knight can escape via b4.} 13...Nb4 {We retreat the knight. White can try to trick us with 14.Qb5+, forking the king and knight, but we would have 14...Nc6 15.Rxc6 Qd7 and we escape by the skin of our teeth!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White's Main Setup / Study 3: White Plays 6. Qe2 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/f73efe60-d94d-4682-9a60-0ac5644b41f9/studies/a4a27de5-0b07-49b7-af24-f9cecd4ec42e/lines"]

1.e4 {This is the third and final study of this chapter. We'll go over the variations that may arise after 6. Qe2. Master them, and you'll master the system! Let's play 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {White pushes another central pawn. We will prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 2...b6 3.Nf3 {White develops a piece and supports d4. We will fianchetto our light-squared bishop, applying pressure to e4.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White defends e4. We will pin the knight, thus placing e4 under attack once more.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {White defends e4. We will develop our knight, adding another attacker on e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Qe2 {Qe2 adds another defender for White, and we will continue to add pressure, while establishing one of our pawns in the center!} 6...d5 7.e5 {This is a very natural response. White thinks: if the pressure is too much on e4 to handle, just push the pawn to e5! There's less pressure there. We will respond by bringing our attacked knight into the outpost on e4.} 7...Ne4 8.O-O {White gets the king to safety. Additionally, they unpin the c3 knight! We will take on c3, and win a pawn.} 8...Nxc3 9.bxc3 {White recaptures, as they should. We will win a pawn with our bishop, and attack the a1-rook.} 9...Bxc3 10.Ba3 {White can choose to play in a sacrificial style. We will show no fear, and accept it!} 10...Bxa1 11.Rxa1 {White recaptures, and we develop the knight, getting ready to go Qd7 and 0-0-0.} 11...Nc6 12.h3 {This move gives the king some breathing room. Our plan proceeds uninterrupted.} 12...Qd7 13.c3 {Establishing a central pawn chain.} 13...O-O-O {We managed to get our king to safety and castle. It's clear that White has no compensation for the exchange. We have solid pawn cover and there is no meaningful attack on the king to show for the sacrificed material.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White's Main Setup / Study 3: White Plays 6. Qe2 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/f73efe60-d94d-4682-9a60-0ac5644b41f9/studies/a4a27de5-0b07-49b7-af24-f9cecd4ec42e/lines"]

1.e4 {This is the third and final study of this chapter. We'll go over the variations that may arise after 6. Qe2. Master them, and you'll master the system! Let's play 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {White pushes another central pawn. We will prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 2...b6 3.Nf3 {White develops a piece and supports d4. We will fianchetto our light-squared bishop, applying pressure to e4.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White defends e4. We will pin the knight, thus placing e4 under attack once more.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {White defends e4. We will develop our knight, adding another attacker on e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Qe2 {Qe2 adds another defender for White, and we will continue to add pressure, while establishing one of our pawns in the center!} 6...d5 7.e5 {This is a very natural response. White thinks: if the pressure is too much on e4 to handle, just push the pawn to e5! There's less pressure there. We will respond by bringing our attacked knight into the outpost on e4.} 7...Ne4 8.O-O {White gets the king to safety. Additionally, they unpin the c3 knight! We will take on c3, and win a pawn.} 8...Nxc3 9.bxc3 {White recaptures, as they should. We will win a pawn with our bishop, and attack the a1-rook.} 9...Bxc3 10.Bg5 {White can try an In-between move! The rook remains en prise, but our queen, which is more valuable, is under attack. We will sidestep the threat from the bishop.} 10...Qd7 11.Rab1 {White rescues their rook.} 11...Nc6 {We're a pawn up and we have pressure on d4. We will still have to be careful on the kingside. A future ...h6 may be a useful prophylactic move.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White's Main Setup / Study 3: White Plays 6. Qe2 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/f73efe60-d94d-4682-9a60-0ac5644b41f9/studies/a4a27de5-0b07-49b7-af24-f9cecd4ec42e/lines"]

1.e4 {This is the third and final study of this chapter. We'll go over the variations that may arise after 6. Qe2. Master them, and you'll master the system! Let's play 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {White pushes another central pawn. We will prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 2...b6 3.Nf3 {White develops a piece and supports d4. We will fianchetto our light-squared bishop, applying pressure to e4.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White defends e4. We will pin the knight, thus placing e4 under attack once more.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {White defends e4. We will develop our knight, adding another attacker on e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Qe2 {Qe2 adds another defender for White, and we will continue to add pressure, while establishing one of our pawns in the center!} 6...d5 7.e5 {This is a very natural response. White thinks: if the pressure is too much on e4 to handle, just push the pawn to e5! There's less pressure there. We will respond by bringing our attacked knight into the outpost on e4.} 7...Ne4 8.Bd2 {White breaks the pin with this move: now three pieces attack the e4-knight. We will solve the problem by capturing on d2.} 8...Nxd2 9.Qxd2 {White recaptures. We will redeploy the bishop. The pin wasn't so useful anymore as the pressure on e4 has been resolved, and now Black guards the g5 square with bishop and queen to make castling safer.} 9...Be7 10.O-O {Getting the king to safety.} 10...c5 {We will strike at the base of the central pawn chain, and we are happy to have the bishop pair.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White's Main Setup / Study 3: White Plays 6. Qe2 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/f73efe60-d94d-4682-9a60-0ac5644b41f9/studies/a4a27de5-0b07-49b7-af24-f9cecd4ec42e/lines"]

1.e4 {This is the third and final study of this chapter. We'll go over the variations that may arise after 6. Qe2. Master them, and you'll master the system! Let's play 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {White pushes another central pawn. We will prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 2...b6 3.Nf3 {White develops a piece and supports d4. We will fianchetto our light-squared bishop, applying pressure to e4.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White defends e4. We will pin the knight, thus placing e4 under attack once more.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {White defends e4. We will develop our knight, adding another attacker on e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Qe2 {Qe2 adds another defender for White, and we will continue to add pressure, while establishing one of our pawns in the center!} 6...d5 7.e5 {This is a very natural response. White thinks: if the pressure is too much on e4 to handle, just push the pawn to e5! There's less pressure there. We will respond by bringing our attacked knight into the outpost on e4.} 7...Ne4 8.Bd2 {White breaks the pin with this move: now three pieces attack the e4-knight. We will solve the problem by capturing on d2.} 8...Nxd2 9.Nxd2 {Taking with the knight is also an option. We will strike at the center with greater potency now that the knight no longer defends the d4-pawn.} 9...c5 10.Qg4 {White attacks g7.} 10...O-O {We can castle safely here as there is no danger of a Bxh7 sacrifice.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White's Main Setup / Study 3: White Plays 6. Qe2 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/f73efe60-d94d-4682-9a60-0ac5644b41f9/studies/a4a27de5-0b07-49b7-af24-f9cecd4ec42e/lines"]

1.e4 {This is the third and final study of this chapter. We'll go over the variations that may arise after 6. Qe2. Master them, and you'll master the system! Let's play 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {White pushes another central pawn. We will prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 2...b6 3.Nf3 {White develops a piece and supports d4. We will fianchetto our light-squared bishop, applying pressure to e4.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White defends e4. We will pin the knight, thus placing e4 under attack once more.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {White defends e4. We will develop our knight, adding another attacker on e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Qe2 {Qe2 adds another defender for White, and we will continue to add pressure, while establishing one of our pawns in the center!} 6...d5 7.e5 {This is a very natural response. White thinks: if the pressure is too much on e4 to handle, just push the pawn to e5! There's less pressure there. We will respond by bringing our attacked knight into the outpost on e4.} 7...Ne4 8.Bxe4 {This looks natural and resolves the tension on c3, but it isn't particularly good. We will take on e4.} 8...dxe4 9.Ng5 {The knight goes to g5 but now it is no longer defending the d4-pawn.} 9...Qxd4 {We are now a healthy pawn up, and we are attacking both c3 and e5.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White's Main Setup / Study 3: White Plays 6. Qe2 / Variation 12"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/f73efe60-d94d-4682-9a60-0ac5644b41f9/studies/a4a27de5-0b07-49b7-af24-f9cecd4ec42e/lines"]

1.e4 {This is the third and final study of this chapter. We'll go over the variations that may arise after 6. Qe2. Master them, and you'll master the system! Let's play 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {White pushes another central pawn. We will prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 2...b6 3.Nf3 {White develops a piece and supports d4. We will fianchetto our light-squared bishop, applying pressure to e4.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White defends e4. We will pin the knight, thus placing e4 under attack once more.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {White defends e4. We will develop our knight, adding another attacker on e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Qe2 {Qe2 adds another defender for White, and we will continue to add pressure, while establishing one of our pawns in the center!} 6...d5 7.Bg5 {This is another way to deal with the pressure on e4. White pins our knight to the queen, reducing its central influence. We will capture on e4 and get a good position.} 7...dxe4 8.Bxe4 {This is the only reasonable recapture. We will continue by taking on e4 with our bishop.} 8...Bxe4 9.Bxf6 {As White would lose after 9.Qxe4 Nxe4 10.Bxd8 Nxc3, removing the knight defending our bishop on e4 is the best option for our opponent.} 9...Qd5 {We defend our strong bishop! White's knight on c3 isn't attacking us since it is pinned by the bishop on b4, and White will have to waste time saving the f6-bishop. Note that 10.Bxg7 Rg8 doesn't help White, as the g2-pawn will fall.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White's Nf3 Setups / Study 1: White Pushes 5. e5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/5daa84a0-4a64-45c3-aa10-e0db9552bb1b/studies/5602040b-1ec0-4084-980b-0aa8fbd72c6c/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 2! This chapter deals with e4/d4, but instead of White supporting them with both knights, the pawns will be supported by a Nf3 and Bd3 configuration. Let's play 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {A second pawn joins the center, and we prepare the fianchetto.} 2...b6 3.Bd3 {White might go 3.Nf3 first, but it's the same idea if they go 4.Bd3 after. 3.Bd3 supports e4. We will continue by fianchettoing the bishop, targeting the e4-pawn.} 3...Bb7 4.Nf3 {White develops the knight. We will stake our claim to the center with ...d5.} 4...d5 5.e5 {By far White's most popular response is to advance the pawn, gaining space and reducing the central pressure. We will once again strike at the center! It's important to move our c-pawn before developing a knight to c6.} 5...c5 6.c3 {Supporting the center. We will play Ne7. Our idea to put the e7-knight on c6, and use the other knight on b8 to support a trade of the light-squared bishops with Ba6.} 6...Ne7 7.O-O {Castling the king into safety. We will continue our plan with the e7-knight.} 7...Nec6 8.Be3 {White develops with Be3, supporting the center. In response we will prepare to castle by playing Be7.} 8...Be7 9.Nbd2 {White develops their final minor piece, and we will castle.} 9...O-O 10.Re1 {White places the rook on a more active square, and gives the bishop the possibility of retreating to c2 or b1 if a trade is offered. We will develop our last minor piece, and bring it where it supports the center, in particular our c5-pawn.} 10...Nd7 11.h3 {White likes this move in these structures. It can be seen as a useful waiting move. We will gain queenside space and make the b4 expansion idea harder for White!} 11...a5 12.a4 {This puts a stop to any further queenside pawn expansion by us. But now an exchange of pawns will lead to a potential knight outpost on b4!} 12...cxd4 13.cxd4 {White recaptures.} 13...Rc8 {We place our rook on the open file before considering a knight jump to b4. Things look promising for Black.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White's Nf3 Setups / Study 1: White Pushes 5. e5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/5daa84a0-4a64-45c3-aa10-e0db9552bb1b/studies/5602040b-1ec0-4084-980b-0aa8fbd72c6c/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 2! This chapter deals with e4/d4, but instead of White supporting them with both knights, the pawns will be supported by a Nf3 and Bd3 configuration. Let's play 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {A second pawn joins the center, and we prepare the fianchetto.} 2...b6 3.Bd3 {White might go 3.Nf3 first, but it's the same idea if they go 4.Bd3 after. 3.Bd3 supports e4. We will continue by fianchettoing the bishop, targeting the e4-pawn.} 3...Bb7 4.Nf3 {White develops the knight. We will stake our claim to the center with ...d5.} 4...d5 5.e5 {By far White's most popular response is to advance the pawn, gaining space and reducing the central pressure. We will once again strike at the center! It's important to move our c-pawn before developing a knight to c6.} 5...c5 6.c3 {Supporting the center. We will play Ne7. Our idea to put the e7-knight on c6, and use the other knight on b8 to support a trade of the light-squared bishops with Ba6.} 6...Ne7 7.O-O {Castling the king into safety. We will continue our plan with the e7-knight.} 7...Nec6 8.Re1 {One major option is 8.Re1, where White supports their center, and places the rook on a more active file. Also, White can retain the bishops when a trade is offered with Ba6 - as we shall see.} 8...Ba6 9.Bc2 {A retreating move, preserving the bishop, made possible because of 8.Re1. We continue with a developing move which also controls g5 and prepares castling.} 9...Be7 10.a3 {White prepares a future b4 queenside expansion. In reply, we bring our last undeveloped minor piece into the game.} 10...Nd7 11.Nbd2 {White continues to develop their pieces. In response, we play a very creative idea! The queenside is somewhat locked. If cxd4, White could recapture with the c-pawn and have two pawns in the center. As Black, we will opt for expanding on the kingside, and activating our rook through that expansion.} 11...g5 12.g4 {If White doesn't like the idea of us playing g4 ourselves... We can push forward with h5, forcing the issue on the kingside.} 12...h5 13.h3 {White defends the pawn, but in the process they have weakened their king's position.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White's Nf3 Setups / Study 1: White Pushes 5. e5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/5daa84a0-4a64-45c3-aa10-e0db9552bb1b/studies/5602040b-1ec0-4084-980b-0aa8fbd72c6c/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 2! This chapter deals with e4/d4, but instead of White supporting them with both knights, the pawns will be supported by a Nf3 and Bd3 configuration. Let's play 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {A second pawn joins the center, and we prepare the fianchetto.} 2...b6 3.Bd3 {White might go 3.Nf3 first, but it's the same idea if they go 4.Bd3 after. 3.Bd3 supports e4. We will continue by fianchettoing the bishop, targeting the e4-pawn.} 3...Bb7 4.Nf3 {White develops the knight. We will stake our claim to the center with ...d5.} 4...d5 5.e5 {By far White's most popular response is to advance the pawn, gaining space and reducing the central pressure. We will once again strike at the center! It's important to move our c-pawn before developing a knight to c6.} 5...c5 6.c3 {Supporting the center. We will play Ne7. Our idea to put the e7-knight on c6, and use the other knight on b8 to support a trade of the light-squared bishops with Ba6.} 6...Ne7 7.O-O {Castling the king into safety. We will continue our plan with the e7-knight.} 7...Nec6 8.a3 {In this variation, the rook is left on f1. White decides to prepare b4 as rapidly as possible. However, since the rook is still on f1, the bishop trade offer on a6 will enforce the removal of the light-squared bishops.} 8...Ba6 9.Bxa6 {White didn't have the option of retreating to c2 because the d3-bishop is pinned to the rook. Thus the bishops are exchanged.} 9...Nxa6 10.b4 {The move prepared by a3. Watch out, a pawn fork of the knights is now threatened! Therefore we will retreat the a6-knight.} 10...Nab8 11.b5 {b5 is played anyway, attacking the other knight, which we will deploy to a5, eyeing the outpost on c4.} 11...Na5 12.Be3 {White develops with Be3, supporting the center.} 12...Be7 {We prepare castling while covering the g5 square. White is a touch weak on the light squares on the queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White's Nf3 Setups / Study 2: White Takes 5.exd5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/5daa84a0-4a64-45c3-aa10-e0db9552bb1b/studies/20598aad-eab3-419d-9174-32c7afc466d7/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study, we'll analyze what happens after we play 4...d5 and White decides to take. It's a very natural response, so let's take a look at it! As before we continue with e6 and b6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {A second pawn joins the center, and we will prepare our fianchetto.} 2...b6 3.Bd3 {White might go 3.Nf3 first, but it's the same idea if they go 4.Bd3 after. 3.Bd3 supports e4. We fianchetto our bishop, putting pressure on e4.} 3...Bb7 4.Nf3 {White develops the knight. We will push in the center.} 4...d5 5.exd5 {This is the second most popular choice by White. Let's explore what happens. We will develop the queen! Additionally, we leave the diagonal open for our bishop.} 5...Qxd5 6.c4 {Attacking the queen! We will retreat to d8.} 6...Qd8 7.O-O {Getting the king to safety. We will develop our king's knight, and stake a claim to the central light squares.} 7...Nf6 8.Nc3 {White develops their other knight. We will bring our f8-bishop out, and get ready to castle.} 8...Be7 9.Re1 {White places their rook actively on a semi-open file. We will castle into safety.} 9...O-O 10.Bf4 {Alternatively, White can develop the bishop to f4. We will continue by developing and placing pressure on d4.} 10...Nc6 11.a3 {This move keeps the knight from b4. White is not currently concerned about the d-pawn, because there are tricks with a Bxh7 discovery. We will offer to trade off the strong bishop on f4.} 11...Bd6 12.Bg5 {Bg5 choosing to pin the knight and not trade. White perceives that we are slightly cramped, so it makes sense for them to keep pieces on. We will now target the bishop with our pawns.} 12...h6 13.Bh4 {Choosing to maintain the pin, and we will reply by harassing the bishop and breaking the pin.} 13...g5 14.Bg3 {White retreats, and we will finally trade bishops.} 14...Bxg3 15.hxg3 {Recapturing towards the center. We will now put our king on a safer square, one where there are no tricks available to the d3-bishop. This means we will finally be threatening to capture twice on d4. With the king still on g8, the sequence of captures on d4 would result in us losing our queen to Bh7+ at the end of the line. With the king on g7, Bh7 is no longer check.} 15...Kg7 16.Bc2 {Retreating the bishop ensures that d4 is now defended adequately.} 16...g4 {But we can kick the knight away, with the idea of removing the defender of d4. White has questions to answer!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White's Nf3 Setups / Study 2: White Takes 5.exd5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/5daa84a0-4a64-45c3-aa10-e0db9552bb1b/studies/20598aad-eab3-419d-9174-32c7afc466d7/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study, we'll analyze what happens after we play 4...d5 and White decides to take. It's a very natural response, so let's take a look at it! As before we continue with e6 and b6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {A second pawn joins the center, and we will prepare our fianchetto.} 2...b6 3.Bd3 {White might go 3.Nf3 first, but it's the same idea if they go 4.Bd3 after. 3.Bd3 supports e4. We fianchetto our bishop, putting pressure on e4.} 3...Bb7 4.Nf3 {White develops the knight. We will push in the center.} 4...d5 5.exd5 {This is the second most popular choice by White. Let's explore what happens. We will develop the queen! Additionally, we leave the diagonal open for our bishop.} 5...Qxd5 6.c4 {Attacking the queen! We will retreat to d8.} 6...Qd8 7.O-O {Getting the king to safety. We will develop our king's knight, and stake a claim to the central light squares.} 7...Nf6 8.Nc3 {White develops their other knight. We will bring our f8-bishop out, and get ready to castle.} 8...Be7 9.Re1 {White places their rook actively on a semi-open file. We will castle into safety.} 9...O-O 10.Bg5 {One option is to develop the bishop to g5. We will develop our b8-knight, supporting its partner on f6.} 10...Nbd7 11.Ne5 {Jumping into the center. We will exchange off the dominating knight.} 11...Nxe5 12.dxe5 {White recaptures. We will fall back, eyeing e5 and c5, while offering a bishop exchange.} 12...Nd7 13.Bxe7 {White exchanges bishops, and we will recapture.} 13...Qxe7 14.Qh5 {Converging on h7. Whatever you do, don't blunder mate! We will neutralize the threat by playing h6.} 14...h6 15.Re3 {Bringing another piece into the mix. Another purpose of h6! Black can offer a queen trade here to neutralize the attack.} 15...Qg5 16.Qxg5 {Accepting the trade, and we recapture.} 16...hxg5 17.b4 {Expanding on the queenside.} 17...a5 {Attacking b4! The idea is that c5 is a strong outpost for the knight, so if Black can remove the b4 pawn, the knight will gain access.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White's Nf3 Setups / Study 2: White Takes 5.exd5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/5daa84a0-4a64-45c3-aa10-e0db9552bb1b/studies/20598aad-eab3-419d-9174-32c7afc466d7/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study, we'll analyze what happens after we play 4...d5 and White decides to take. It's a very natural response, so let's take a look at it! As before we continue with e6 and b6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {A second pawn joins the center, and we will prepare our fianchetto.} 2...b6 3.Bd3 {White might go 3.Nf3 first, but it's the same idea if they go 4.Bd3 after. 3.Bd3 supports e4. We fianchetto our bishop, putting pressure on e4.} 3...Bb7 4.Nf3 {White develops the knight. We will push in the center.} 4...d5 5.exd5 {This is the second most popular choice by White. Let's explore what happens. We will develop the queen! Additionally, we leave the diagonal open for our bishop.} 5...Qxd5 6.O-O {White castles into safety. We will develop the knight, building control over the central light squares.} 6...Nf6 7.Nc3 {Attacking our queen! We will retreat to d8.} 7...Qd8 8.Bg5 {This is the most popular continuation, pinning the knight. We will reply with the natural ...Be7, breaking the pin.} 8...Be7 9.Qe2 {Qe2 connects the rooks and looks down the more active e-file.} 9...O-O {Now we get the king to safety. Play might continue with developing the last minor piece and pushing c7-c5.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups with 1. e4 / Study 1: White Setups with Bd3 and Nd2, Ne2 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/e393fce0-447a-4cf5-8b97-b1d234a963a0/studies/453687d6-9e93-4c3f-8996-775d32ff9b6b/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study we start off with variations where White develops the light-squared bishop first, defending the e4 pawn. The point will be to analyze the various knight moves available to White in the 4th move, which we haven't looked at yet. Let's continue with 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {Establishing a second central pawn. And we will continue with our fianchetto system.} 2...b6 3.Bd3 {The bishop arrives behind the pawns, supporting e4. We will fianchetto our bishop, aiming at the center.} 3...Bb7 4.Ne2 {Another option would be this move, developing another knight and supporting d4. We will strike at the center!} 4...d5 5.e5 {White pushes forward, gaining space. We will strike at the base of the pawn chain.} 5...c5 6.c3 {White defends their pawn chain with the c-pawn. We will play Ne7, and we can utilize ideas from Chapter 2, like bringing the e-knight to c6, and offering the bishop trade on a6.} 6...Ne7 7.O-O {White castles into safety. We will reply with Nec6, getting the knight to a better square and freeing our dark-squared bishop.} 7...Nec6 8.Be3 {This is a sound move, developing and supporting the center. We will offer a trade of bishops. If White rejects, there'll be a pin on the knight on e2.} 8...Ba6 9.Bc2 {Rejecting the trade. Our bishop is well-placed on that diagonal. We continue to develop.} 9...Nd7 10.Nd2 {White also develops. We place the rook on c8, preparing to activate it in the near future when we open the c-file.} 10...Rc8 11.Nf3 {The knight makes more sense here, supporting d4 and also allowing the queen to have sight of d4. Let's exchange on d4, opening the file for our rook.} 11...cxd4 12.cxd4 {Recapturing and defending e5. We play Na5, with the idea of jumping to the outpost on c4 and attacking both the e3-bishop and the pawn on b2.} 12...Na5 13.b3 {And if White wants to defend against the knight jump?} 13...Qc7 {We converge strongly on the c-file! b3 also weakened a3, so now our bishop can go there!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups with 1. e4 / Study 1: White Setups with Bd3 and Nd2, Ne2 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/e393fce0-447a-4cf5-8b97-b1d234a963a0/studies/453687d6-9e93-4c3f-8996-775d32ff9b6b/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study we start off with variations where White develops the light-squared bishop first, defending the e4 pawn. The point will be to analyze the various knight moves available to White in the 4th move, which we haven't looked at yet. Let's continue with 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {Establishing a second central pawn. And we will continue with our fianchetto system.} 2...b6 3.Bd3 {The bishop arrives behind the pawns, supporting e4. We will fianchetto our bishop, aiming at the center.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White develops, also supporting e4. We will develop our king's knight and continue to eye the e4-pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nge2 {White develops another knight, supporting their c3-knight and the d4-pawn. We will push in the center.} 5...d5 6.e5 {White chooses to take space, attacking our knight on f6. We will retreat the knight, planning to later redeploy it to e7.} 6...Ng8 7.O-O {White castles the king into safety, and we will strike at the center with c5.} 7...c5 8.Nf4 {A very aggressive move! d4 isn't really a free pawn. White can recover it later with Nce2. With that in mind, we prefer to continue by putting pressure on d4 by developing the b8-knight.} 8...Nc6 9.dxc5 {If White chooses to resolve the tension, we will recapture with the bishop.} 9...Bxc5 10.Qg4 {White develops their queen, attacking g7. We will push the g-pawn forward, constructing a defensive shield of pawns.} 10...g6 11.Re1 {White places their rook on a more active square, and supports their pawn on e5.} 11...Nge7 {We develop the knight. The plan is to go ...a6, to prevent Nb5 jumps, and we can expand further with ...b5 next.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups with 1. e4 / Study 1: White Setups with Bd3 and Nd2, Ne2 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/e393fce0-447a-4cf5-8b97-b1d234a963a0/studies/453687d6-9e93-4c3f-8996-775d32ff9b6b/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study we start off with variations where White develops the light-squared bishop first, defending the e4 pawn. The point will be to analyze the various knight moves available to White in the 4th move, which we haven't looked at yet. Let's continue with 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {Establishing a second central pawn. And we will continue with our fianchetto system.} 2...b6 3.Bd3 {The bishop arrives behind the pawns, supporting e4. We will fianchetto our bishop, aiming at the center.} 3...Bb7 4.Nd2 {White can develop with this move, also supporting e4. We will play to undermine the white center.} 4...c5 5.c3 {White plays c3, supporting d4. We will develop our king's knight, attacking more forces to the attack on e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Ngf3 {Developing the knight and supporting the center. We exchange on d4.} 6...cxd4 7.cxd4 {White recaptures with the pawn. If White recaptures with a piece, this helps Black, due to the diminished central presence. We will develop our bishop and prepare to castle kingside.} 7...Be7 8.O-O {Getting the king to safety. We offer the bishop trade. White's bishop is pointing dangerously at the kingside.} 8...Ba6 9.Bxa6 {The trade is accepted. White has little choice, because if the bishop retreated, the f1-rook would be en prise. We recapture.} 9...Nxa6 10.a3 {White covers the b4 square, preventing our knight from arriving there.} 10...O-O {We castle into safety. We can play to occupy the open c-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups with 1. e4 / Study 1: White Setups with Bd3 and Nd2, Ne2 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/e393fce0-447a-4cf5-8b97-b1d234a963a0/studies/453687d6-9e93-4c3f-8996-775d32ff9b6b/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study we start off with variations where White develops the light-squared bishop first, defending the e4 pawn. The point will be to analyze the various knight moves available to White in the 4th move, which we haven't looked at yet. Let's continue with 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {Establishing a second central pawn. And we will continue with our fianchetto system.} 2...b6 3.Bd3 {The bishop arrives behind the pawns, supporting e4. We will fianchetto our bishop, aiming at the center.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White develops, also supporting e4. We will develop our king's knight and continue to eye the e4-pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nge2 {White develops another knight, supporting their c3-knight and the d4-pawn. We will push in the center.} 5...d5 6.e5 {White chooses to take space, attacking our knight on f6. We will retreat the knight, planning to later redeploy it to e7.} 6...Ng8 7.O-O {White castles the king into safety, and we will strike at the center with c5.} 7...c5 8.dxc5 {Alternatively, White might simply resolve the tension. In this case, we will recapture towards the center, controlling more squares.} 8...bxc5 9.b3 {White prevents c4, which would have trapped the bishop on d3. We will develop our king's knight.} 9...Ne7 10.Ng3 {White opens up lines for their queen, and gives themselves the option of pushing forward with the f-pawn. We will develop our other knight.} 10...Nd7 11.f4 {Expanding on the kingside! We will need to cut across White's plan by playing ...g6.} 11...g6 12.Nb5 {With the crude idea of giving a checkmate on d6!} 12...Nf5 {And we defend! Our knight reaches a nice outpost that in turn stops the kingside expansion and offers a trade which would leave us with a very solid central pawn mass if White accepts.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups with 1. e4 / Study 1: White Setups with Bd3 and Nd2, Ne2 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/e393fce0-447a-4cf5-8b97-b1d234a963a0/studies/453687d6-9e93-4c3f-8996-775d32ff9b6b/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study we start off with variations where White develops the light-squared bishop first, defending the e4 pawn. The point will be to analyze the various knight moves available to White in the 4th move, which we haven't looked at yet. Let's continue with 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.d4 {Establishing a second central pawn. And we will continue with our fianchetto system.} 2...b6 3.Bd3 {The bishop arrives behind the pawns, supporting e4. We will fianchetto our bishop, aiming at the center.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White develops, also supporting e4. We will develop our king's knight and continue to eye the e4-pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.Nge2 {White develops another knight, supporting their c3-knight and the d4-pawn. We will push in the center.} 5...d5 6.e5 {White chooses to take space, attacking our knight on f6. We will retreat the knight, planning to later redeploy it to e7.} 6...Ng8 7.O-O {White castles the king into safety, and we will strike at the center with c5.} 7...c5 8.Nf4 {A very aggressive move! d4 isn't really a free pawn. White can recover it later with Nce2. With that in mind, we prefer to continue by putting pressure on d4 by developing the b8-knight.} 8...Nc6 9.Qh5 {White could try this venomous move, threatening to take on e6, as the f-pawn is pinned. We will defend e6 with our queen.} 9...Qd7 10.dxc5 {White can play this move, reducing the pressure on d4. We will reply by pushing the queen away before recapturing.} 10...g6 11.Qh3 {Choosing to stay on the kingside, applying pressure there.} 11...Bxc5 {And we develop the bishop. As you can see, castling queenside is available to us in the next move.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups with 1. e4 / Study 2: Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/e393fce0-447a-4cf5-8b97-b1d234a963a0/studies/591a095a-4622-4e01-8362-485241fe26e2/lines"]

1.e4 {This study will cover things we don't expect to see after the common 1.e4 e6 2.d4 b6. What if White plays moves like 3.f4, or 3.Nc3 and 4.a3? Well, study this chapter, and you'll be able to deal with some off-beat approaches by White.} 1...e6 2.d4 {A second central pawn push. Nothing out of the ordinary yet. We will prepare to fianchetto.} 2...b6 3.Nc3 {Supporting e4. This is a very natural move. Nothing odd here. We will continue with our normal fianchetto.} 3...Bb7 4.f3 {How about this solution? White just supports e4 with another pawn! We will push d5, striking at the white center.} 4...d5 5.e5 {White closes off the center. The f-pawn looks misplaced, blocking the natural development square of the g1-knight. We will break with c5.} 5...c5 6.Be3 {White develops their bishop, supporting d4. We will continue to pressurize the d-pawn.} 6...Nc6 7.Bb5 {White tries to neutralize the pressure by pinning our attacker. We support our knight on c6. The e7-knight can go to f5 next move, attacking d4 and e3 as well!} 7...Ne7 8.Nge2 {Developing the last minor piece.} 8...Nf5 {We place the white center under pressure.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups with 1. e4 / Study 2: Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/e393fce0-447a-4cf5-8b97-b1d234a963a0/studies/591a095a-4622-4e01-8362-485241fe26e2/lines"]

1.e4 {This study will cover things we don't expect to see after the common 1.e4 e6 2.d4 b6. What if White plays moves like 3.f4, or 3.Nc3 and 4.a3? Well, study this chapter, and you'll be able to deal with some off-beat approaches by White.} 1...e6 2.d4 {A second central pawn push. Nothing out of the ordinary yet. We will prepare to fianchetto.} 2...b6 3.Nc3 {Supporting e4. This is a very natural move. Nothing odd here. We will continue with our normal fianchetto.} 3...Bb7 4.a3 {With a3, White prevents us from playing our traditional Bb4 pinning the knight on c3. The knight, therefore, will stay supporting e4, unbothered. Let's develop and continue attacking e4.} 4...Nf6 5.e5 {White may elect to push the pawn. This is a very natural continuation, so, while it isn't the most popular, it's still useful for us to get to know. We will jump into the center and offer a trade. We're threatening to double White's pawns!} 5...Nd5 6.Nxd5 {That is why more often than not White chooses to take. We will recapture with the bishop, keeping the long diagonal open.} 6...Bxd5 7.c4 {White can attack our bishop. We will retreat it back to its natural home on b7.} 7...Bb7 8.Nf3 {White develops, supporting their center. We will reply with d6, aiming to break up the strong pawn center.} 8...d6 9.Bf4 {White supports e5. We will continue increasing the pressure.} 9...Nc6 10.exd6 {And if White takes...} 10...Bxd6 {We'll recapture, developing our bishop and offering a trade. We're comfortable here, with a powerful bishop on b7.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups with 1. e4 / Study 2: Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/e393fce0-447a-4cf5-8b97-b1d234a963a0/studies/591a095a-4622-4e01-8362-485241fe26e2/lines"]

1.e4 {This study will cover things we don't expect to see after the common 1.e4 e6 2.d4 b6. What if White plays moves like 3.f4, or 3.Nc3 and 4.a3? Well, study this chapter, and you'll be able to deal with some off-beat approaches by White.} 1...e6 2.d4 {A second central pawn push. Nothing out of the ordinary yet. We will prepare to fianchetto.} 2...b6 3.Nf3 {What if White continues in a regular fashion as we have seen before, by developing a knight supporting the center? As usual, we fianchetto our bishop, attacking the pawn on e4.} 3...Bb7 4.Nc3 {White defends e4. Two knights, developing as normal. We will bring our king's bishop out, and pin the knight, thus placing pressure on e4 once more.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd2 {This is a natural move, breaking the pin! But is that a free pawn we see? We will take on c3, removing the defender.} 5...Bxc3 6.Bxc3 {White recaptures with the bishop. Now we can take on e4.} 6...Bxe4 7.d5 {White pushes d5. This tricky move opens up an attack on g7! Look at the bishop! If g7 falls, so will the rook behind it!} 7...Nf6 {We block the diagonal by developing a piece and we are a healthy pawn up.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups with 1. e4 / Study 2: Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/e393fce0-447a-4cf5-8b97-b1d234a963a0/studies/591a095a-4622-4e01-8362-485241fe26e2/lines"]

1.e4 {This study will cover things we don't expect to see after the common 1.e4 e6 2.d4 b6. What if White plays moves like 3.f4, or 3.Nc3 and 4.a3? Well, study this chapter, and you'll be able to deal with some off-beat approaches by White.} 1...e6 2.d4 {A second central pawn push. Nothing out of the ordinary yet. We will prepare to fianchetto.} 2...b6 3.Bd3 {White may decide to support e4 like this. Nothing we haven't seen before, should White choose to go 4.Nf3 in the next move. But what if they continue with a different idea? In the meantime, we fianchetto our bishop.} 3...Bb7 4.Be3 {White can opt for a center supported by their two bishops. Remember that whenever the white knight doesn't develop to c3, we won't play Bb4 as there will be nothing to pin. Therefore we develop the bishop on e7, with the idea of castling kingside.} 4...Be7 5.Nf3 {White develops, supporting the center. We will develop, with our pieces converging on e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Nc3 {White adds a defender to e4. It seems White studied a book on opening principles, as the pieces are all developed supporting the center! However, we are familiar with the principles of undermining a center!} 6...c5 7.dxc5 {White releases the tension, and we will capture towards the center with the b-pawn, exerting control over d4.} 7...bxc5 8.O-O {White castles into safety.} 8...O-O {We do likewise. We have no problems as we emerge from the opening.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups with 1. e4 / Study 2: Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/e393fce0-447a-4cf5-8b97-b1d234a963a0/studies/591a095a-4622-4e01-8362-485241fe26e2/lines"]

1.e4 {This study will cover things we don't expect to see after the common 1.e4 e6 2.d4 b6. What if White plays moves like 3.f4, or 3.Nc3 and 4.a3? Well, study this chapter, and you'll be able to deal with some off-beat approaches by White.} 1...e6 2.d4 {A second central pawn push. Nothing out of the ordinary yet. We will prepare to fianchetto.} 2...b6 3.f4 {White might have learned that having a large center is good! In this case, we must remember that large centers can be attacked with pawns. We begin our counterstrike by fianchettoing.} 3...Bb7 4.Bd3 {White defends the e-pawn. White hasn't placed a knight on c3, therefore there's no point in playing Bb4 next move, as white could simply play c3. With that in mind, we will strike at the center with the c-pawn instead.} 4...c5 5.c3 {White wants to hold on to the center, naturally. We will exchange on d4.} 5...cxd4 6.cxd4 {White recaptures. We will place further pressure on the center by developing our g8-knight.} 6...Nf6 7.Nc3 {White defends e4. We will apply further pressure by pinning the knight.} 7...Bb4 8.Qe2 {Since the knight is pinned, White defends the e-pawn further with Qe2. We will continue to challenge the center, this time by pushing our d-pawn.} 8...d5 9.e5 {White pushes the pawn and attacks our knight!} 9...Ne4 {We jump into the center, since our knight is sufficiently supported. We are attacking c3. Furthermore, if white takes, they'll relinquish their light-square bishop, and our queen will gain a tempo on the d4 pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups with 1. e4 / Study 2: Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/e393fce0-447a-4cf5-8b97-b1d234a963a0/studies/591a095a-4622-4e01-8362-485241fe26e2/lines"]

1.e4 {This study will cover things we don't expect to see after the common 1.e4 e6 2.d4 b6. What if White plays moves like 3.f4, or 3.Nc3 and 4.a3? Well, study this chapter, and you'll be able to deal with some off-beat approaches by White.} 1...e6 2.d4 {A second central pawn push. Nothing out of the ordinary yet. We will prepare to fianchetto.} 2...b6 3.Nc3 {Supporting e4. This is a very natural move. Nothing odd here. We will continue with our normal fianchetto.} 3...Bb7 4.a3 {With a3, White prevents us from playing our traditional Bb4 pinning the knight on c3. The knight, therefore, will stay supporting e4, unbothered. Let's develop and continue attacking e4.} 4...Nf6 5.Bd3 {White defends e4. Let's strike at the center with c5!} 5...c5 6.Nf3 {White develops, defending d4. We will again put pressure on the center, this time by pushing d5.} 6...d5 7.e5 {White advances, gaining space and attacking our knight. We jump into the center, supported by the d5-pawn and our bishop on b7.} 7...Ne4 8.dxc5 {White releases the pressure in the center. We will play an intermezzo, exchanging knights and doubling White's pawns.} 8...Nxc3 9.bxc3 {White of course recaptures. We will capture towards the center, covering the d4 and b4 squares.} 9...bxc5 10.Rb1 {The rook moves to b-the open file, attacking our bishop. We will simply move it to c6.} 10...Bc6 11.c4 {White advances c4, with the option of undoubling their c-pawns.} 11...Nd7 {We develop the last queenside piece. After a future ...Be7, we'll castle into safety, with a solid position.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups with 1. e4 / Study 2: Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/e393fce0-447a-4cf5-8b97-b1d234a963a0/studies/591a095a-4622-4e01-8362-485241fe26e2/lines"]

1.e4 {This study will cover things we don't expect to see after the common 1.e4 e6 2.d4 b6. What if White plays moves like 3.f4, or 3.Nc3 and 4.a3? Well, study this chapter, and you'll be able to deal with some off-beat approaches by White.} 1...e6 2.d4 {A second central pawn push. Nothing out of the ordinary yet. We will prepare to fianchetto.} 2...b6 3.Nc3 {Supporting e4. This is a very natural move. Nothing odd here. We will continue with our normal fianchetto.} 3...Bb7 4.a3 {With a3, White prevents us from playing our traditional Bb4 pinning the knight on c3. The knight, therefore, will stay supporting e4, unbothered. Let's develop and continue attacking e4.} 4...Nf6 5.Bd3 {White defends e4. Let's strike at the center with c5!} 5...c5 6.Nf3 {White develops, defending d4. We will again put pressure on the center, this time by pushing d5.} 6...d5 7.exd5 {White decides to release the tension. We will recapture with our knight, not with the pawn, which would voluntarily block our light-square bishop.} 7...Nxd5 8.Nxd5 {White decides to exchange. We recapture with our queen, bringing it to an aggressive central location.} 8...Qxd5 9.c4 {Our queen is attacked! We retreat, keeping an eye on the f4-square. White can no longer develop their dark-squared bishop there.} 9...Qd6 10.dxc5 {White again chooses to liquidate. We will recapture towards the center.} 10...bxc5 11.O-O {Getting the king to safety.} 11...Be7 {We prepare to castle, too. We have a healthy position, with clear open lines for our pieces.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White's 3 Pawn Center / Study 1: Introduction To White's Options / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/2499dcf1-c7d1-4a3f-bdf1-019968feecb4/studies/7ab639a6-c4a5-4972-b724-aa771b2c22dc/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome! In this chapter, we'll learn about what happens when White opens with 1.d4, and then proceeds to arrange a large center with c4 and e4. You'll also see that White supports this center with a piece like Nc3 or Bd3. This time, we'll start off with 1...b6.} 1...b6 2.c4 {This move brings out another pawn which controls the central d5 square. We proceed with our plan to fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, which controls d5 and e4. Now we prepare to counter on the light-squares with ...e6, controlling d5. This move additionally opens up our dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.e4 {White gains space by putting another pawn in the center! An impressive center that we'll have fun undermining. We begin by pinning the knight, thus loosening e4. This introduces the first checkpoint of our study. White has four concrete responses they can throw at us.} 4...Bb4 5.Qc2 {White can choose to defend e4 this way. We will bring out our queen, attacking e4 again.} 5...Qh4 6.Bd3 {White, therefore, adds another defender. We will strike at the center with ...f5, noting that the g-pawn is loose if White captures on f5.} 6...f5 7.Nf3 {Nf3 gains a tempo on our queen. Before we move the queen to safety, we will insert the capture on c3, with check.} 7...Bxc3+ 8.Kf1 {The cunning Kf1 is possible here, defending g2 and getting out of the pin, while maintaining an attack on both the queen on h4 and the bishop on c3. We will retreat to the safety of e7.} 8...Qe7 9.bxc3 {White recaptures with the b-pawn, leaving the queen in the defense of e4. We will reply by exchanging in the center.} 9...Bxe4 10.Bxe4 {White removes our strong bishop, and we recapture.} 10...fxe4 11.Qxe4 {White regains the pawn, hitting the rook on a8. We will develop our queen's knight, blocking the attack.} 11...Nc6 12.Bg5 {Developing the bishop, attacking the queen! We block the attack with our other knight, attacking the White queen.} 12...Nf6 13.Bxf6 {White snaps off the knight, and we recapture with the queen.} 13...Qxf6 14.d5 {A clever move! Attacking our knight, trying to remove it from c6 to unleash an attack on our a8-rook. We will move the knight to e7, and sacrifice a pawn.} 14...Ne7 15.dxe6 {White opens up the attack on our rook! We don't have time to recapture. We will just move the rook instead, protecting d7.} 15...Rd8 16.exd7+ {White goes pawn-grabbing.} 16...Rxd7 {We just recapture. We have a safer king and better piece development, while White's c-pawns are very weak.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White's 3 Pawn Center / Study 1: Introduction To White's Options / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/2499dcf1-c7d1-4a3f-bdf1-019968feecb4/studies/7ab639a6-c4a5-4972-b724-aa771b2c22dc/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome! In this chapter, we'll learn about what happens when White opens with 1.d4, and then proceeds to arrange a large center with c4 and e4. You'll also see that White supports this center with a piece like Nc3 or Bd3. This time, we'll start off with 1...b6.} 1...b6 2.c4 {This move brings out another pawn which controls the central d5 square. We proceed with our plan to fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, which controls d5 and e4. Now we prepare to counter on the light-squares with ...e6, controlling d5. This move additionally opens up our dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.e4 {White gains space by putting another pawn in the center! An impressive center that we'll have fun undermining. We begin by pinning the knight, thus loosening e4. This introduces the first checkpoint of our study. White has four concrete responses they can throw at us.} 4...Bb4 5.f3 {f3 is a sound move, defending the e4 pawn with another pawn. But we can still counter it with f5!} 5...f5 6.exf5 {White takes, and thus gets their pawn removed from the center. This is good. In the resulting positions, you'll notice, White's center looks less impressive than a few moves ago. We will reply with the sharp ...Nh6, applying pressure on f5, with the idea of Qh4+ if the bishop takes our knight on h6.} 6...Nh6 7.fxe6 {White can choose to pawn grab. We'll choose to improve our pieces with Nf5! You'll notice we now threaten Qh4+ because our knight covers g3.} 7...Nf5 8.Bf4 {Master level players typically develop another piece, as they're behind in development. This move also adds another defender to the g3 square. After that, we can just recapture the pawn, supporting our active knight on f5.} 8...dxe6 9.Qa4+ {White could opt to check us, forking our king and bishop. We will interpose our b8-knight and protect the bishop.} 9...Nc6 10.d5 {White pushes, attacking our knight. We make it easier to fend off the attack by removing a defender of d5.} 10...Bxc3+ 11.bxc3 {White recaptures, and we'll capture on d5.} 11...exd5 12.O-O-O {White castles, pinning our pawn to the queen! But we will castle into safety, and unpin the c6-knight.} 12...O-O 13.cxd5 {White captures our pawn, attacking our knight.} 13...Na5 {And we sidestep, opening our bishop up to attack the pawn. While we're in no rush to take it since our bishop would end up in a precarious situation, White still has to be careful. We have five developed pieces, whereas white only has 3. The defenses of the White king are also somewhat scattered.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White's 3 Pawn Center / Study 1: Introduction To White's Options / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/2499dcf1-c7d1-4a3f-bdf1-019968feecb4/studies/7ab639a6-c4a5-4972-b724-aa771b2c22dc/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome! In this chapter, we'll learn about what happens when White opens with 1.d4, and then proceeds to arrange a large center with c4 and e4. You'll also see that White supports this center with a piece like Nc3 or Bd3. This time, we'll start off with 1...b6.} 1...b6 2.c4 {This move brings out another pawn which controls the central d5 square. We proceed with our plan to fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, which controls d5 and e4. Now we prepare to counter on the light-squares with ...e6, controlling d5. This move additionally opens up our dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.e4 {White gains space by putting another pawn in the center! An impressive center that we'll have fun undermining. We begin by pinning the knight, thus loosening e4. This introduces the first checkpoint of our study. White has four concrete responses they can throw at us.} 4...Bb4 5.f3 {f3 is a sound move, defending the e4 pawn with another pawn. But we can still counter it with f5!} 5...f5 6.exf5 {White takes, and thus gets their pawn removed from the center. This is good. In the resulting positions, you'll notice, White's center looks less impressive than a few moves ago. We will reply with the sharp ...Nh6, applying pressure on f5, with the idea of Qh4+ if the bishop takes our knight on h6.} 6...Nh6 7.fxe6 {White can choose to pawn grab. We'll choose to improve our pieces with Nf5! You'll notice we now threaten Qh4+ because our knight covers g3.} 7...Nf5 8.exd7+ {Other players choose to gobble up another pawn. We will recapture, and develop another minor piece. We play in gambit style!} 8...Nxd7 9.Bf4 {White develops a piece (at last). And we complete our development by castling (all we need to do now is connect rooks).} 9...O-O 10.Ne2 {White might have anxiety because they notice they're down in development. Also, they might try to block the e-file to protect their king. We will play a queen check, and probe at the enemy king.} 10...Qh4+ 11.g3 {With g3, White blocks the check. We drop back with tempo, attacking f3.} 11...Qh5 12.Bg2 {White tries to support f3.} 12...Bxf3 {But we can still capture the pawn! We have a very promising position.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White's 3 Pawn Center / Study 1: Introduction To White's Options / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/2499dcf1-c7d1-4a3f-bdf1-019968feecb4/studies/7ab639a6-c4a5-4972-b724-aa771b2c22dc/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome! In this chapter, we'll learn about what happens when White opens with 1.d4, and then proceeds to arrange a large center with c4 and e4. You'll also see that White supports this center with a piece like Nc3 or Bd3. This time, we'll start off with 1...b6.} 1...b6 2.c4 {This move brings out another pawn which controls the central d5 square. We proceed with our plan to fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, which controls d5 and e4. Now we prepare to counter on the light-squares with ...e6, controlling d5. This move additionally opens up our dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.e4 {White gains space by putting another pawn in the center! An impressive center that we'll have fun undermining. We begin by pinning the knight, thus loosening e4. This introduces the first checkpoint of our study. White has four concrete responses they can throw at us.} 4...Bb4 5.f3 {f3 is a sound move, defending the e4 pawn with another pawn. But we can still counter it with f5!} 5...f5 6.e5 {White might choose to advance the pawn. We will develop our queen's knight. Our development is ahead of White's.} 6...Nc6 7.Be3 {They develop the bishop, supporting d4.} 7...d6 {And we undermine the center. White may well find that they have over-extended.} *

