1.d4 {Welcome! In this chapter, we'll learn about what happens when White opens with 1.d4, and then proceeds to arrange a large center with c4 and e4. You'll also see that White supports this center with a piece like Nc3 or Bd3. This time, we'll start off with 1...b6.} 1...b6 2.c4 {This move brings out another pawn which controls the central d5 square. We proceed with our plan to fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, which controls d5 and e4. Now we prepare to counter on the light-squares with ...e6, controlling d5. This move additionally opens up our dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.e4 {White gains space by putting another pawn in the center! An impressive center that we'll have fun undermining. We begin by pinning the knight, thus loosening e4. This introduces the first checkpoint of our study. White has four concrete responses they can throw at us.} 4...Bb4 5.Qc2 {White can choose to defend e4 this way. We will bring out our queen, attacking e4 again.} 5...Qh4 6.Bd3 {White, therefore, adds another defender. We will strike at the center with ...f5, noting that the g-pawn is loose if White captures on f5.} 6...f5 7.Nf3 {Nf3 gains a tempo on our queen. Before we move the queen to safety, we will insert the capture on c3, with check.} 7...Bxc3+ 8.Kf1 {The cunning Kf1 is possible here, defending g2 and getting out of the pin, while maintaining an attack on both the queen on h4 and the bishop on c3. We will retreat to the safety of e7.} 8...Qe7 9.bxc3 {White recaptures with the b-pawn, leaving the queen in the defense of e4. We will reply by exchanging in the center.} 9...Bxe4 10.Bxe4 {White removes our strong bishop, and we recapture.} 10...fxe4 11.Qxe4 {White regains the pawn, hitting the rook on a8. We will develop our queen's knight, blocking the attack.} 11...Nc6 12.Be3 {A passive developing move.} 12...Nf6 {Nf6 allows us to develop with a tempo.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White's 3 Pawn Center / Study 1: Introduction To White's Options / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/2499dcf1-c7d1-4a3f-bdf1-019968feecb4/studies/7ab639a6-c4a5-4972-b724-aa771b2c22dc/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome! In this chapter, we'll learn about what happens when White opens with 1.d4, and then proceeds to arrange a large center with c4 and e4. You'll also see that White supports this center with a piece like Nc3 or Bd3. This time, we'll start off with 1...b6.} 1...b6 2.c4 {This move brings out another pawn which controls the central d5 square. We proceed with our plan to fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, which controls d5 and e4. Now we prepare to counter on the light-squares with ...e6, controlling d5. This move additionally opens up our dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.e4 {White gains space by putting another pawn in the center! An impressive center that we'll have fun undermining. We begin by pinning the knight, thus loosening e4. This introduces the first checkpoint of our study. White has four concrete responses they can throw at us.} 4...Bb4 5.d5 {d5 blocks the attack on e4! Our bishop now faces the pawn on d5, and its scope is blunted. We will capture on c3, doubling White's pawns.} 5...Bxc3+ 6.bxc3 {White recaptures the piece.} 6...Qe7 {Now we develop the queen on an active square, covering a3 before a bishop chooses to make its way there. Additionally, we're opposing the king with our queen, so if we can take exd5 next move, our queen will have an open attack on the undefended e4 pawn!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White's 3 Pawn Center / Study 1: Introduction To White's Options / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/2499dcf1-c7d1-4a3f-bdf1-019968feecb4/studies/7ab639a6-c4a5-4972-b724-aa771b2c22dc/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome! In this chapter, we'll learn about what happens when White opens with 1.d4, and then proceeds to arrange a large center with c4 and e4. You'll also see that White supports this center with a piece like Nc3 or Bd3. This time, we'll start off with 1...b6.} 1...b6 2.c4 {This move brings out another pawn which controls the central d5 square. We proceed with our plan to fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, which controls d5 and e4. Now we prepare to counter on the light-squares with ...e6, controlling d5. This move additionally opens up our dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.e4 {White gains space by putting another pawn in the center! An impressive center that we'll have fun undermining. We begin by pinning the knight, thus loosening e4. This introduces the first checkpoint of our study. White has four concrete responses they can throw at us.} 4...Bb4 5.f3 {f3 is a sound move, defending the e4 pawn with another pawn. But we can still counter it with f5!} 5...f5 6.exf5 {White takes, and thus gets their pawn removed from the center. This is good. In the resulting positions, you'll notice, White's center looks less impressive than a few moves ago. We will reply with the sharp ...Nh6, applying pressure on f5, with the idea of Qh4+ if the bishop takes our knight on h6.} 6...Nh6 7.fxe6 {White can choose to pawn grab. We'll choose to improve our pieces with Nf5! You'll notice we now threaten Qh4+ because our knight covers g3.} 7...Nf5 8.Bf4 {Master level players typically develop another piece, as they're behind in development. This move also adds another defender to the g3 square. After that, we can just recapture the pawn, supporting our active knight on f5.} 8...dxe6 9.Ne2 {White tries to catch up in development.} 9...O-O {But we're so far ahead that our king gets to safety, while White's is still stuck in the middle of the board. We'll develop our queenside pieces, connect the rooks and put pressure on d4.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White's 3 Pawn Center / Study 1: Introduction To White's Options / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/2499dcf1-c7d1-4a3f-bdf1-019968feecb4/studies/7ab639a6-c4a5-4972-b724-aa771b2c22dc/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome! In this chapter, we'll learn about what happens when White opens with 1.d4, and then proceeds to arrange a large center with c4 and e4. You'll also see that White supports this center with a piece like Nc3 or Bd3. This time, we'll start off with 1...b6.} 1...b6 2.c4 {This move brings out another pawn which controls the central d5 square. We proceed with our plan to fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, which controls d5 and e4. Now we prepare to counter on the light-squares with ...e6, controlling d5. This move additionally opens up our dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.e4 {White gains space by putting another pawn in the center! An impressive center that we'll have fun undermining. We begin by pinning the knight, thus loosening e4. This introduces the first checkpoint of our study. White has four concrete responses they can throw at us.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {This is the main line, defending e4.} 5...f5 {We proceed by undermining the center! Note that White cannot take because the g2-pawn would become loose. The resulting variations that can occur after 5...f5 will be analyzed in the next study. For now, just remember the basic moves to get here!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White's 3 Pawn Center / Study 1: Introduction To White's Options / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/2499dcf1-c7d1-4a3f-bdf1-019968feecb4/studies/7ab639a6-c4a5-4972-b724-aa771b2c22dc/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome! In this chapter, we'll learn about what happens when White opens with 1.d4, and then proceeds to arrange a large center with c4 and e4. You'll also see that White supports this center with a piece like Nc3 or Bd3. This time, we'll start off with 1...b6.} 1...b6 2.c4 {This move brings out another pawn which controls the central d5 square. We proceed with our plan to fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, which controls d5 and e4. Now we prepare to counter on the light-squares with ...e6, controlling d5. This move additionally opens up our dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.e4 {White gains space by putting another pawn in the center! An impressive center that we'll have fun undermining. We begin by pinning the knight, thus loosening e4. This introduces the first checkpoint of our study. White has four concrete responses they can throw at us.} 4...Bb4 5.Qc2 {White can choose to defend e4 this way. We will bring out our queen, attacking e4 again.} 5...Qh4 6.Bd3 {White, therefore, adds another defender. We will strike at the center with ...f5, noting that the g-pawn is loose if White captures on f5.} 6...f5 7.Nf3 {Nf3 gains a tempo on our queen. Before we move the queen to safety, we will insert the capture on c3, with check.} 7...Bxc3+ 8.Qxc3 {If the queen recaptures...} 8...Qg4 {We can sidestep, attacking g2.} 9.O-O {White gets to safety. In the resulting position, we can capture on e4, challenging White to find the correct moves to not lose material.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White's 3 Pawn Center / Study 2: Big Center Variation (Bd3, Nc3) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/2499dcf1-c7d1-4a3f-bdf1-019968feecb4/studies/ebc687f6-c986-4db4-9642-e6ef4a8e7e59/lines"]

1.d4 {Now that you've been introduced to positions after White opens with 1.d4, let's analyze what happens after 1.d4 b6 2.c4 Bb7 3.Nc3 e6 4.e4 Bb4 5.Bd3 f5. There are many moves that can result, and some of these lines are tricky. Learn them, and you learn the system! We'll start with 1...b6.} 1...b6 2.c4 {This move brings another pawn to a location controlling central squares. We will fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, which controls d5 and e4. We will counter on the light squares by controlling d5 at the very least, and open up our dark-squared bishop's route to b4.} 3...e6 4.e4 {White gains space by putting another pawn in the center! An impressive center that we'll have fun undermining. We began the attack on the center with Bb4, pinning the c3-knight.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {This is the main line, defending e4. We will strike at the big pawn center with ...f5, remembering that g2 is tender.} 5...f5 6.Qe2 {White can choose to defend e4 with the queen. We will place further pressure on the e-pawn.} 6...Nf6 7.Bg5 {Pinning the knight to avoid neutralize some of our pressure. We will initiate exchanges on e4.} 7...fxe4 8.Bxe4 {Exchanges continue on e4.} 8...Bxe4 9.Bxf6 {A valuable intermezzo, removing the defender of our bishop on e4. White needed to do this prior to recapturing on e4, otherwise they would come out of the sequence material down. We will recapture on f6.} 9...Qxf6 10.Qxe4 {The queen takes back the bishop, attacking our rook. We will ignore it, and castle! If White takes on a8, we will capture on f2 and White's position will collapse.} 10...O-O 11.Nf3 {White blocks the attack on f2. We now have to solve the problem of our attacked rook, and we do this by blocking the line of fire with the knight.} 11...Nc6 12.O-O {White gets their king into safety, and we will capture on c3, doubling their pawns.} 12...Bxc3 13.bxc3 {White recaptures the only way possible. We will offer a queen trade, relying on our superior pawn structure in an endgame.} 13...Qf5 14.Qe2 {Choosing to retain the queens. We will activate our queen's rook, with a potential ...e5 pawn push in mind.} 14...Rae8 15.Rfe1 {White seeks to prevent e5. We will support it by playing ...d6.} 15...d6 16.Qb2 {White maneuvers the queen around, planning to challenge our queen with a future Qb5. We bring our knight to the kingside.} 16...Ne7 17.a4 {Seeking to maybe go a5 and open up a line for the a1-rook. We continue to relocate the knight on the kingside.} 17...Ng6 18.Qb5 {White now wants a queen trade to improve their structure! We won't cooperate. We play c5, blocking the line between the opposing queens.} 18...c5 19.Qb1 {White continues to offer a queen trade. Our presence on the kingside has got stronger over the past few moves. We will retain queens, because we are now playing for the attack.} 19...Qh5 20.Qe4 {Trying to control squares on the kingside.} 20...Nf4 {Our knight becomes a monster on f4! Our rook on f8 also looks down the file menacingly! White is very much on the defensive.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White's 3 Pawn Center / Study 2: Big Center Variation (Bd3, Nc3) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/2499dcf1-c7d1-4a3f-bdf1-019968feecb4/studies/ebc687f6-c986-4db4-9642-e6ef4a8e7e59/lines"]

1.d4 {Now that you've been introduced to positions after White opens with 1.d4, let's analyze what happens after 1.d4 b6 2.c4 Bb7 3.Nc3 e6 4.e4 Bb4 5.Bd3 f5. There are many moves that can result, and some of these lines are tricky. Learn them, and you learn the system! We'll start with 1...b6.} 1...b6 2.c4 {This move brings another pawn to a location controlling central squares. We will fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, which controls d5 and e4. We will counter on the light squares by controlling d5 at the very least, and open up our dark-squared bishop's route to b4.} 3...e6 4.e4 {White gains space by putting another pawn in the center! An impressive center that we'll have fun undermining. We began the attack on the center with Bb4, pinning the c3-knight.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {This is the main line, defending e4. We will strike at the big pawn center with ...f5, remembering that g2 is tender.} 5...f5 6.Qc2 {White can choose to support e4 via a Qc2/Bd3 battery. As so often before, we will apply further pressure to the center by developing our knight on f6.} 6...Nf6 7.f3 {f3 serves to protect e4 and block the diagonal to g2. We'll develop the other knight, attacking d4.} 7...Nc6 8.Ne2 {The knight develops to e2, supporting their comrade on c3. We will soften up the white center by exchanging pawns on e4.} 8...fxe4 9.fxe4 {White recaptures, and we will castle, placing our rook on the open f-file. If White wants to try 9...0-0 10.e5 to win h7, they may have trouble defending their king after 10.Ng4.} 9...O-O 10.O-O {White can play the very natural move of castling. We will jump our knight into action on g4! The e2-knight is not best placed defensively.} 10...Ng4 11.Rxf8+ {White seeks to alleviate the tension... We recapture and threaten to infiltrate with the queen!} 11...Qxf8 12.Nd1 {It was vital to cover the f2 entry point.} 12...Qf6 {But we go to f6, eyeing up the d4-pawn and with the possibility to continue attacking with Qh4. White is under a lot of pressure. Look at all those pieces stuck on the back rank!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White's 3 Pawn Center / Study 2: Big Center Variation (Bd3, Nc3) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/2499dcf1-c7d1-4a3f-bdf1-019968feecb4/studies/ebc687f6-c986-4db4-9642-e6ef4a8e7e59/lines"]

1.d4 {Now that you've been introduced to positions after White opens with 1.d4, let's analyze what happens after 1.d4 b6 2.c4 Bb7 3.Nc3 e6 4.e4 Bb4 5.Bd3 f5. There are many moves that can result, and some of these lines are tricky. Learn them, and you learn the system! We'll start with 1...b6.} 1...b6 2.c4 {This move brings another pawn to a location controlling central squares. We will fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, which controls d5 and e4. We will counter on the light squares by controlling d5 at the very least, and open up our dark-squared bishop's route to b4.} 3...e6 4.e4 {White gains space by putting another pawn in the center! An impressive center that we'll have fun undermining. We began the attack on the center with Bb4, pinning the c3-knight.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {This is the main line, defending e4. We will strike at the big pawn center with ...f5, remembering that g2 is tender.} 5...f5 6.d5 {White can play to block the long diagonal. We will start by capturing on e4, undermining the pawn defense of d5.} 6...fxe4 7.Bxe4 {White recaptures. We continue by developing a piece, attacking the bishop. We could also have considered ...Qh4.} 7...Nf6 8.Bf3 {White retreats. We will take on c3, damaging the pawn structure.} 8...Bxc3+ 9.bxc3 {White recaptures, and we will castle.} 9...O-O 10.Ne2 {White develops the knight to e2 since the bishop is already on f3. We will push forward with e5. We are even threatening to trap their bishop with e4!} 10...e5 11.Ng3 {White makes room for the bishop to retreat, and guards the e4 square. We will simply strengthen our central point on e5 by playing ...d6.} 11...d6 12.O-O {White castles into safety, and we continue to develop.} 12...Nbd7 13.Be3 {The bishop goes to e3, and we prepare to line up our major pieces on the half-open f-file.} 13...Rf7 14.Qc2 {The queen is developed onto a better diagonal.} 14...Qf8 {We double major pieces on the f-file. We have a promising position. White's bishops don't have much scope, and our knights will prove flexible.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White's 3 Pawn Center / Study 2: Big Center Variation (Bd3, Nc3) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/2499dcf1-c7d1-4a3f-bdf1-019968feecb4/studies/ebc687f6-c986-4db4-9642-e6ef4a8e7e59/lines"]

1.d4 {Now that you've been introduced to positions after White opens with 1.d4, let's analyze what happens after 1.d4 b6 2.c4 Bb7 3.Nc3 e6 4.e4 Bb4 5.Bd3 f5. There are many moves that can result, and some of these lines are tricky. Learn them, and you learn the system! We'll start with 1...b6.} 1...b6 2.c4 {This move brings another pawn to a location controlling central squares. We will fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, which controls d5 and e4. We will counter on the light squares by controlling d5 at the very least, and open up our dark-squared bishop's route to b4.} 3...e6 4.e4 {White gains space by putting another pawn in the center! An impressive center that we'll have fun undermining. We began the attack on the center with Bb4, pinning the c3-knight.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {This is the main line, defending e4. We will strike at the big pawn center with ...f5, remembering that g2 is tender.} 5...f5 6.exf5 {Our bishop was looking at g2, but what if White captures on f5 anyway? We gobble up the g2-pawn, with the rook to follow!} 6...Bxg2 7.Qh5+ {White plays all-out attack! We move the king to the side.} 7...Kf8 8.fxe6 {Don't take the rook yet! White threatens mate! We had better recapture on e6.} 8...dxe6 9.Ne2 {White chooses to develop. White wants to bring the knight to f4, where it will threaten to fork our king and queen. We develop with gain of time. We do not need to take the rook immediately.} 9...Nf6 10.Qh4 {The queen retreats, pinning our knight. But now we have time to finally capture the rook.} 10...Bxh1 11.Bg5 {Placing pressure on f6. We will exchange a pair of minor pieces to reduce White's attacking potential.} 11...Bxc3+ 12.bxc3 {White recaptures with the b-pawn. We will support our pinned knight with the king, also defending e6 against the coming Nf4 idea.} 12...Kf7 13.Kd2 {White opens up an attack on our bishop. We retreat it back to its natural base on b7.} 13...Bb7 14.Nf4 {An attempt from White to attack our kingside. We will bring out our last undeveloped minor piece.} 14...Nbd7 15.Re1 {White tries piling up on e6.} 15...Nf8 {We fall back, defending with the knight. We've survived the attack, and White is down material.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White's 3 Pawn Center / Study 2: Big Center Variation (Bd3, Nc3) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/2499dcf1-c7d1-4a3f-bdf1-019968feecb4/studies/ebc687f6-c986-4db4-9642-e6ef4a8e7e59/lines"]

1.d4 {Now that you've been introduced to positions after White opens with 1.d4, let's analyze what happens after 1.d4 b6 2.c4 Bb7 3.Nc3 e6 4.e4 Bb4 5.Bd3 f5. There are many moves that can result, and some of these lines are tricky. Learn them, and you learn the system! We'll start with 1...b6.} 1...b6 2.c4 {This move brings another pawn to a location controlling central squares. We will fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, which controls d5 and e4. We will counter on the light squares by controlling d5 at the very least, and open up our dark-squared bishop's route to b4.} 3...e6 4.e4 {White gains space by putting another pawn in the center! An impressive center that we'll have fun undermining. We began the attack on the center with Bb4, pinning the c3-knight.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {This is the main line, defending e4. We will strike at the big pawn center with ...f5, remembering that g2 is tender.} 5...f5 6.Qh5+ {Maybe White will give the obvious check. It is not particularly effective. We will just block with the pawn.} 6...g6 7.Qe2 {White retreats, defending the e-pawn. We will develop our knight, as before exerting influence on the e4 square.} 7...Nf6 8.Bg5 {White pins our knight to our queen. We will initiate exchanges on e4.} 8...fxe4 9.Bxe4 {White recaptures, as we were threatening the bishop. We will capture on e4 with the bishop.} 9...Bxe4 10.Bxf6 {White needs to insert this capture before taking on e4 with the queen. We recapture on f6.} 10...Qxf6 11.Qxe4 {White takes the bishop, hitting our a8-rook. We will block with the knight.} 11...Nc6 12.Nf3 {White develops the knight and defends d4. We will damage White's pawn structure and argue that we are a touch better.} 12...Bxc3+ 13.bxc3 {White recaptures.} 13...O-O {And we castle, bringing the rook to the semi-open f-file! We have a more compact pawn structure.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White's 3 Pawn Center / Study 2: Big Center Variation (Bd3, Nc3) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/2499dcf1-c7d1-4a3f-bdf1-019968feecb4/studies/ebc687f6-c986-4db4-9642-e6ef4a8e7e59/lines"]

1.d4 {Now that you've been introduced to positions after White opens with 1.d4, let's analyze what happens after 1.d4 b6 2.c4 Bb7 3.Nc3 e6 4.e4 Bb4 5.Bd3 f5. There are many moves that can result, and some of these lines are tricky. Learn them, and you learn the system! We'll start with 1...b6.} 1...b6 2.c4 {This move brings another pawn to a location controlling central squares. We will fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, which controls d5 and e4. We will counter on the light squares by controlling d5 at the very least, and open up our dark-squared bishop's route to b4.} 3...e6 4.e4 {White gains space by putting another pawn in the center! An impressive center that we'll have fun undermining. We began the attack on the center with Bb4, pinning the c3-knight.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {This is the main line, defending e4. We will strike at the big pawn center with ...f5, remembering that g2 is tender.} 5...f5 6.f3 {f3 defends e4 with the pawn. Let's take a moment here to reflect our options. We actually have quite a bit of leeway here. Nc6 and Qh4+ are options. Let's go with the latter.} 6...Qh4+ 7.g3 {White plays g3, attacking our queen. We will retreat her to h5, eyeing the f3-pawn.} 7...Qh5 8.exf5 {A natural capture. White has enough pieces protecting f3, and they might see this trade as beneficial. We will playing in gambit style and develop!} 8...Nf6 9.Bf4 {White decides not to fall further behind in development, and brings out a piece with Bf4. We will castle, placing our rook on an active file in the process.} 9...O-O 10.fxe6 {Should White persist with pawn grabs...} 10...dxe6 {We just recapture. We have better development! We have five active pieces, while White only has three, and the white king is still in the center.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White's 3 Pawn Center / Study 2: Big Center Variation (Bd3, Nc3) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/2499dcf1-c7d1-4a3f-bdf1-019968feecb4/studies/ebc687f6-c986-4db4-9642-e6ef4a8e7e59/lines"]

1.d4 {Now that you've been introduced to positions after White opens with 1.d4, let's analyze what happens after 1.d4 b6 2.c4 Bb7 3.Nc3 e6 4.e4 Bb4 5.Bd3 f5. There are many moves that can result, and some of these lines are tricky. Learn them, and you learn the system! We'll start with 1...b6.} 1...b6 2.c4 {This move brings another pawn to a location controlling central squares. We will fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, which controls d5 and e4. We will counter on the light squares by controlling d5 at the very least, and open up our dark-squared bishop's route to b4.} 3...e6 4.e4 {White gains space by putting another pawn in the center! An impressive center that we'll have fun undermining. We began the attack on the center with Bb4, pinning the c3-knight.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {This is the main line, defending e4. We will strike at the big pawn center with ...f5, remembering that g2 is tender.} 5...f5 6.Qc2 {White can choose to support e4 via a Qc2/Bd3 battery. As so often before, we will apply further pressure to the center by developing our knight on f6.} 6...Nf6 7.Bg5 {This move, played with the idea of pinning our knight, actually loses material. We simply take on e4 with the pawn.} 7...fxe4 8.Bxf6 {White should accept that they've simply lost a pawn, but if they try Bxf6 attacking our queen (which worked in previous variations)...} 8...exd3 {We have this unpleasant intermezzo! We take the d3-bishop, attacking White's queen. Keep in mind that our light-squared bishop is now also aiming at g2.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White's 3 Pawn Center / Study 3: Big Center, Without Knights / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/2499dcf1-c7d1-4a3f-bdf1-019968feecb4/studies/ee0f6fdc-37bb-4d8d-81c9-92636598f2d5/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the final study of this chapter! We'll be looking at how to counter White if they choose to place three pawns in the center and a bishop on d3 supporting e4. We'll start with 1...b6.} 1...b6 2.e4 {White adds another pawn to the center! We fianchetto, attacking e4.} 2...Bb7 3.Bd3 {White defends the e4-pawn with the bishop. We play e6, staking a claim to the center and opening up our other bishop.} 3...e6 4.c4 {Now White grabs more central space by pushing a third pawn. We can proceed by giving a check. As you'll see, White will have to block with a piece or lose castling rights!} 4...Bb4+ 5.Bd2 {Bd2 is a very logical move, blocking the check and attacking the bishop. We will exchange bishops.} 5...Bxd2+ 6.Nxd2 {White recaptures with the knight to develop another minor piece. In this position Jobava and Mamedyarov have both played 6...d6. We shall do likewise! This allows us to control some central dark squares after trading the dark-squared bishops.} 6...d6 7.Ngf3 {White chooses to develop, supporting the d4-pawn. We develop our knight, supporting a future e5 pawn break.} 7...Nd7 8.O-O {White gets to safety. We will break with e5.} 8...e5 9.d5 {White can opt to close down the center.} 9...a5 {We can play a5, placing another pawn on a dark-square, and securing an outpost for our knight on c5 in the future. Note that our bishop is better than the white counterpart, due to the pawn configuration.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White's 3 Pawn Center / Study 3: Big Center, Without Knights / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/2499dcf1-c7d1-4a3f-bdf1-019968feecb4/studies/ee0f6fdc-37bb-4d8d-81c9-92636598f2d5/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the final study of this chapter! We'll be looking at how to counter White if they choose to place three pawns in the center and a bishop on d3 supporting e4. We'll start with 1...b6.} 1...b6 2.e4 {White adds another pawn to the center! We fianchetto, attacking e4.} 2...Bb7 3.Bd3 {White defends the e4-pawn with the bishop. We play e6, staking a claim to the center and opening up our other bishop.} 3...e6 4.c4 {Now White grabs more central space by pushing a third pawn. We can proceed by giving a check. As you'll see, White will have to block with a piece or lose castling rights!} 4...Bb4+ 5.Kf1 {Kf1 is a bit odd, and not super intuitive at the lower levels. Nevertheless, it's the top engine move. We will simply retreat the bishop to e7. We have spent two moves with our bishop, but ultimately White cannot regain castling rights.} 5...Be7 6.Nc3 {White continues with piece development. Meanwhile we prepare e5 with d6.} 6...d6 7.Nf3 {White develops a kingside piece supporting d4. We will develop our knight, preparing to castle.} 7...Nf6 8.g3 {White's idea is to castle by hand.} 8...O-O {But we can castle immediately! From here on, we'll have the possibility of developing our other knight to d7, then playing e5 for counterplay. We have no problems as we emerge from the opening.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White's 3 Pawn Center / Study 3: Big Center, Without Knights / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/2499dcf1-c7d1-4a3f-bdf1-019968feecb4/studies/ee0f6fdc-37bb-4d8d-81c9-92636598f2d5/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the final study of this chapter! We'll be looking at how to counter White if they choose to place three pawns in the center and a bishop on d3 supporting e4. We'll start with 1...b6.} 1...b6 2.e4 {White adds another pawn to the center! We fianchetto, attacking e4.} 2...Bb7 3.Bd3 {White defends the e4-pawn with the bishop. We play e6, staking a claim to the center and opening up our other bishop.} 3...e6 4.c4 {Now White grabs more central space by pushing a third pawn. We can proceed by giving a check. As you'll see, White will have to block with a piece or lose castling rights!} 4...Bb4+ 5.Nc3 {Voluntarily stepping into a pin. This is White's most popular response.} 5...f5 {This allows us to play f5, attacking e4 and our bishop is pressurizing the long diagonal to g2! This is known territory, as we have examined this position in the previous study.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: White Plays d4 & c4 / Study 1: The d4 & c4 Main Setup / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/6cb135aa-4ae5-4c8a-b156-ef8211cd1138/studies/4ea1ec99-0bbc-4f2b-b2b7-ca945c6e8e14/lines"]

1.d4 {Congratulations on getting this far! You're practically halfway through. In this first study, you'll learn to counter White when they set up with d4/c4/Nc3/Nf3. In order to remember these variations, you can think of them as Two Knights with d4/c4. We will prepare to fianchetto.} 1...b6 2.c4 {Controlling the center through a flank pawn. We will fianchetto our bishop.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White develops a knight, supporting central squares. We play ...e6, staking a claim to the center, and preparing to activate the f8-bishop.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {And White plays Nf3, supporting d4 and controlling e5. We bring the bishop out and pin the knight, as we are used to doing. White has three options which we will explore.} 4...Bb4 5.g3 {Finally, g3 recognizes the value of the diagonal that Black occupied. White wants to fianchetto to counter Black's control of the diagonal. Here we can proceed to damage the pawn structure.} 5...Bxc3+ 6.bxc3 {White recaptures the only way possible. We develop with an eye towards e4.} 6...Nf6 7.Bg2 {White places their bishop on the long diagonal. Let's place a pawn on a dark square, controlling e5 and remembering that we have traded off our dark-squared bishop.} 7...d6 8.O-O {White castles into safety. We will defend our bishop and support a c7-c5 push.} 8...Qc8 9.Re1 {White gets ready to support a pawn push of their own. We will castle.} 9...O-O 10.Qc2 {White plays Qc2. In an ideal world, this would support an e4 pawn push, gaining a big center. We will act first, cutting across these plans, and place our bishop on e4, attacking the white queen.} 10...Be4 11.Qa4 {Getting the queen out of danger. We will play c5, as we planned when we played Qc8! Also, we act to place pawns on dark squares, recognizing that we don't have a dark-squared bishop.} 11...c5 12.Bf1 {White removes the bishop from the diagonal in order to give it scope towards c4 (which is weakly defended). We will complete the development of our minor pieces.} 12...Nc6 13.Bb2 {The pawn position is quite closed. There isn't a great location for White to develop their dark-squared bishop to, but let's say they try b2. We'll play Na5, applying pressure on c4. This is a static weakness for White, and we can play against it.} 13...Na5 14.Nd2 {White retreats to defend c4. But now we can attack with e5!} 14...e5 15.d5 {White goes d5, blocking the center. We can go to f5, creating a bishop and queen battery.} 15...Bf5 16.e4 {If White finally achieves that long-prepared e4 push...} 16...Bh3 {We can use the recently created battery to propose a bishop trade and get to h3 with our queen! White's pieces on the queenside are very inactive at the moment.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: White Plays d4 & c4 / Study 1: The d4 & c4 Main Setup / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/6cb135aa-4ae5-4c8a-b156-ef8211cd1138/studies/4ea1ec99-0bbc-4f2b-b2b7-ca945c6e8e14/lines"]

1.d4 {Congratulations on getting this far! You're practically halfway through. In this first study, you'll learn to counter White when they set up with d4/c4/Nc3/Nf3. In order to remember these variations, you can think of them as Two Knights with d4/c4. We will prepare to fianchetto.} 1...b6 2.c4 {Controlling the center through a flank pawn. We will fianchetto our bishop.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White develops a knight, supporting central squares. We play ...e6, staking a claim to the center, and preparing to activate the f8-bishop.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {And White plays Nf3, supporting d4 and controlling e5. We bring the bishop out and pin the knight, as we are used to doing. White has three options which we will explore.} 4...Bb4 5.Qb3 {Qb3 seems logical, attacking our bishop and defending the c3 knight. We will support the bishop with the a-pawn, and gain a little space on the queenside.} 5...a5 6.a3 {"Ok, that's enough!" says White. Our bishop is attacked with a3. But here we have the very clever reply of ...a4.} 6...a4 7.Qxb4 {White could grab our bishop. It looks like a free piece, but we attack the queen with the knight, and it is very short of squares.} 7...Nc6 8.Qb5 {The only option.} 8...Ra5 {The queen is trapped!} *

[Event "Chapter 5: White Plays d4 & c4 / Study 1: The d4 & c4 Main Setup / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/6cb135aa-4ae5-4c8a-b156-ef8211cd1138/studies/4ea1ec99-0bbc-4f2b-b2b7-ca945c6e8e14/lines"]

1.d4 {Congratulations on getting this far! You're practically halfway through. In this first study, you'll learn to counter White when they set up with d4/c4/Nc3/Nf3. In order to remember these variations, you can think of them as Two Knights with d4/c4. We will prepare to fianchetto.} 1...b6 2.c4 {Controlling the center through a flank pawn. We will fianchetto our bishop.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White develops a knight, supporting central squares. We play ...e6, staking a claim to the center, and preparing to activate the f8-bishop.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {And White plays Nf3, supporting d4 and controlling e5. We bring the bishop out and pin the knight, as we are used to doing. White has three options which we will explore.} 4...Bb4 5.e3 {e3 is also a sound setup, defending the knight on f3 with the queen and deciding that White is not going to worry about Black capturing on c3. We can play f5! We have a strong grip on the light squares.} 5...f5 6.Bd3 {White develops a piece and tries to counter our control of e4. We will continue our development with ...Nf6, and exert further control over the e4 square.} 6...Nf6 7.O-O {White castles. We will do likewise.} 7...O-O 8.Bd2 {White develops their final minor piece, and the bishop is defending c3. We will capture anyway. Our play is all about trying to gain control over e4.} 8...Bxc3 9.Bxc3 {White maintains the integrity of their pawn structure. We will jump our knight into e4.} 9...Ne4 10.Rc1 {After Rc1 to recapture with a piece and not damage the pawns... We can continue with a calm d6, a useful pawn move to gain control over some dark squares. Remember: we no longer have a dark-squared bishop.} 10...d6 11.Nd2 {In positions like this, when one side (in this case Black) has a strong piece in the center, the other side attempts to attack it, to remove it from its strong position. Taking the dark-squared bishop is preferable over taking a misplaced knight on d2.} 11...Nxc3 12.Rxc3 {White recaptures with the rook so as to not damage the pawn structure.} 12...Nd7 {And we develop our final minor piece with ideas of returning to f6 (controlling e4), or pushing e5.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: White Plays d4 & c4 / Study 1: The d4 & c4 Main Setup / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/6cb135aa-4ae5-4c8a-b156-ef8211cd1138/studies/4ea1ec99-0bbc-4f2b-b2b7-ca945c6e8e14/lines"]

1.d4 {Congratulations on getting this far! You're practically halfway through. In this first study, you'll learn to counter White when they set up with d4/c4/Nc3/Nf3. In order to remember these variations, you can think of them as Two Knights with d4/c4. We will prepare to fianchetto.} 1...b6 2.c4 {Controlling the center through a flank pawn. We will fianchetto our bishop.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White develops a knight, supporting central squares. We play ...e6, staking a claim to the center, and preparing to activate the f8-bishop.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {And White plays Nf3, supporting d4 and controlling e5. We bring the bishop out and pin the knight, as we are used to doing. White has three options which we will explore.} 4...Bb4 5.Qb3 {Qb3 seems logical, attacking our bishop and defending the c3 knight. We will support the bishop with the a-pawn, and gain a little space on the queenside.} 5...a5 6.a3 {"Ok, that's enough!" says White. Our bishop is attacked with a3. But here we have the very clever reply of ...a4.} 6...a4 7.Qc2 {White retreats to c2, keeping an eye on the knight. We'll exchange on c3.} 7...Bxc3+ 8.Qxc3 {The queen recaptures to preserve the integrity of the pawn structure. We will develop our g8-knight, controlling some central squares.} 8...Nf6 9.Bg5 {White pins our knight to the queen. We will aim to break the pin with our kingside pawns.} 9...h6 10.Bh4 {White retreats the attacked bishop, but now we will push our g-pawn and decisively break the pin.} 10...g5 11.Bg3 {White retreats the bishop. We can jump into the center with a tempo on the queen. Let's do it!} 11...Ne4 12.Qc2 {White retreats, keeping an eye on our knight on e4.} 12...f5 {We can play f5, supporting our knight on e4! We'll develop the rest of our pieces, and push in the center with ...d6 and ...e5. We have taken control over a lot of space.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: White Plays d4 & c4 / Study 2: The d4 & c4 Sidelines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/6cb135aa-4ae5-4c8a-b156-ef8211cd1138/studies/6b14cdbe-48eb-4422-aa4e-08bd318a5673/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the final study of this chapter! You're becoming quite the e6/b6 expert, aren't you? Well in this chapter we'll continue analyzing what happens after white opens with d4 and c4, but what if they prevent our idea of Bb4 pinning the knight with 4. a3? Here you'll find how to respond! Let's first start with our fianchetto.} 1...b6 2.c4 {White plays c4, controlling d5 from the side. We will develop with ...Bb7 and gain control of the long diagonal.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White's knight comes out, supporting central squares. With ...e6, we prepare to develop our dark-squared bishop. However...} 3...e6 4.a3 {White prevents Bb4 by preemptively playing a3! We will just continue gaining control of e4. Here you'll be introduced to potential responses White might play.} 4...f5 5.Nf3 {With Nf3 White continues normally, with development and central control. We will jump to f6, controlling e4 and d5 secondarily. After this White has four main options.} 5...Nf6 6.d5 {White can choose to gain space with this move! We will bring out our queen's knight, aiming at the c5 square, which is no longer controlled by the white d-pawn.} 6...Na6 7.b4 {White could choose to expand on the queenside, covering the c5 square. We can develop our king's bishop on d6, where it controls both sides of the board.} 7...Bd6 8.g3 {White may seek to fianchetto their light-squared bishop. We will castle.} 8...O-O 9.Bg2 {White fianchettoes their bishop. We will continue by undermining the center!} 9...c6 10.dxc6 {White can choose to capture, in which case we will take back with the pawn. We can't capture with the bishop, because that would walk into a b5 pawn fork.} 10...dxc6 11.O-O {White secures their king.} 11...Qe7 {And we connect rooks, finishing our development. We can contemplate central expansion with ...e5, and bringing our queen's rook to the open d-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: White Plays d4 & c4 / Study 2: The d4 & c4 Sidelines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/6cb135aa-4ae5-4c8a-b156-ef8211cd1138/studies/6b14cdbe-48eb-4422-aa4e-08bd318a5673/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the final study of this chapter! You're becoming quite the e6/b6 expert, aren't you? Well in this chapter we'll continue analyzing what happens after white opens with d4 and c4, but what if they prevent our idea of Bb4 pinning the knight with 4. a3? Here you'll find how to respond! Let's first start with our fianchetto.} 1...b6 2.c4 {White plays c4, controlling d5 from the side. We will develop with ...Bb7 and gain control of the long diagonal.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White's knight comes out, supporting central squares. With ...e6, we prepare to develop our dark-squared bishop. However...} 3...e6 4.a3 {White prevents Bb4 by preemptively playing a3! We will just continue gaining control of e4. Here you'll be introduced to potential responses White might play.} 4...f5 5.Nf3 {With Nf3 White continues normally, with development and central control. We will jump to f6, controlling e4 and d5 secondarily. After this White has four main options.} 5...Nf6 6.Bf4 {Nf3 achieves the same for white: development and central control. We will develop our bishop and prepare to castle.} 6...Be7 7.e3 {White supports d4 with e3. We will castle into safety.} 7...O-O 8.h3 {Play can continue with the very natural h3, giving the bishop a retreat square. Now we can jump to the center, with a positional threat: taking on c3 to damage the pawn structure.} 8...Ne4 9.Rc1 {White defends the threat. We will place our pawn on d6 to blunt the bishop on f4. This also prepares to develop the knight!} 9...d6 10.Be2 {White develops their bishop. They intend to castle. Part of our idea here is to get the knight to f6 to support the e4 knight.} 10...Nd7 11.O-O {White gets their king to safety... And we will support our knight on e4.} 11...Ndf6 12.Qb3 {If the queen comes out to b3, we initiate a trade on c3...} 12...Nxc3 13.Qxc3 {With the idea that when White recaptures...} 13...Ne4 {We make great use of the e4 square with a tempo. From here, we have ideas of piece activation, like Qe8 for Qg6 and also Rd8.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: White Plays d4 & c4 / Study 2: The d4 & c4 Sidelines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/6cb135aa-4ae5-4c8a-b156-ef8211cd1138/studies/6b14cdbe-48eb-4422-aa4e-08bd318a5673/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the final study of this chapter! You're becoming quite the e6/b6 expert, aren't you? Well in this chapter we'll continue analyzing what happens after white opens with d4 and c4, but what if they prevent our idea of Bb4 pinning the knight with 4. a3? Here you'll find how to respond! Let's first start with our fianchetto.} 1...b6 2.c4 {White plays c4, controlling d5 from the side. We will develop with ...Bb7 and gain control of the long diagonal.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White's knight comes out, supporting central squares. With ...e6, we prepare to develop our dark-squared bishop. However...} 3...e6 4.a3 {White prevents Bb4 by preemptively playing a3! We will just continue gaining control of e4. Here you'll be introduced to potential responses White might play.} 4...f5 5.d5 {d5 blocks the diagonal of the b7-bishop and gains space in the center. We can develop with Nf6, applying pressure on d5 and controlling e4.} 5...Nf6 6.g3 {White seeks to deploy their bishop on the h1-a8 diagonal, probably because our bishop is so good on it. Here we will develop our dark-squared bishop flexibly, exerting pressure on both sides of the white position.} 6...Bd6 7.Bg2 {The bishop gets on the coveted diagonal, supporting the strongpoint on d5. We will prepare to jump to c5, from where the knight will eye the important e4 square.} 7...Na6 8.Nf3 {White develops another piece. In response we will castle and get to safety.} 8...O-O 9.O-O {White likewise castles, and our knight hops into c5.} 9...Nc5 10.Nd4 {White wants to bring their knight into the center, too. White is posing questions to our e6-pawn.} 10...Be5 {We jump into the center, applying pressure to the knight on d4. We are threatening Bxb3 and Nb3! We generally want to play ...d6 (If White moves the d4-knight) and ...Qe7 to finish development. As f4 might inconvenience our bishop, we should also capture the knight on c3 if possible. This will disrupt White's pawn structure, and then we can establish a strong knight on c5.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: White Plays d4 & c4 / Study 2: The d4 & c4 Sidelines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/6cb135aa-4ae5-4c8a-b156-ef8211cd1138/studies/6b14cdbe-48eb-4422-aa4e-08bd318a5673/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the final study of this chapter! You're becoming quite the e6/b6 expert, aren't you? Well in this chapter we'll continue analyzing what happens after white opens with d4 and c4, but what if they prevent our idea of Bb4 pinning the knight with 4. a3? Here you'll find how to respond! Let's first start with our fianchetto.} 1...b6 2.c4 {White plays c4, controlling d5 from the side. We will develop with ...Bb7 and gain control of the long diagonal.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White's knight comes out, supporting central squares. With ...e6, we prepare to develop our dark-squared bishop. However...} 3...e6 4.a3 {White prevents Bb4 by preemptively playing a3! We will just continue gaining control of e4. Here you'll be introduced to potential responses White might play.} 4...f5 5.Bf4 {Bf4 is also possible should White choose to develop another piece. We will continue by developing our kingside, controlling key squares.} 5...Nf6 6.Nf3 {Nf3 achieves the same for white: development and central control. We will develop our bishop and prepare to castle.} 6...Be7 7.e3 {White supports d4 with e3. We will castle into safety.} 7...O-O 8.Be2 {Developing the bishop and preparing castling. We will continue with one of our main ideas, and occupy the central e4 square.} 8...Ne4 9.h3 {If white goes for the very natural h3... we will simply exchange on c3 and double White's pawns.} 9...Nxc3 10.bxc3 {White recaptures.} 10...Nc6 {And we develop our final minor piece. We can improve the minor pieces by placing the knight on a5 and the dark-squared bishop on f6.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: White Plays d4 & c4 / Study 2: The d4 & c4 Sidelines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/6cb135aa-4ae5-4c8a-b156-ef8211cd1138/studies/6b14cdbe-48eb-4422-aa4e-08bd318a5673/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the final study of this chapter! You're becoming quite the e6/b6 expert, aren't you? Well in this chapter we'll continue analyzing what happens after white opens with d4 and c4, but what if they prevent our idea of Bb4 pinning the knight with 4. a3? Here you'll find how to respond! Let's first start with our fianchetto.} 1...b6 2.c4 {White plays c4, controlling d5 from the side. We will develop with ...Bb7 and gain control of the long diagonal.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White's knight comes out, supporting central squares. With ...e6, we prepare to develop our dark-squared bishop. However...} 3...e6 4.a3 {White prevents Bb4 by preemptively playing a3! We will just continue gaining control of e4. Here you'll be introduced to potential responses White might play.} 4...f5 5.Nf3 {With Nf3 White continues normally, with development and central control. We will jump to f6, controlling e4 and d5 secondarily. After this White has four main options.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {Maybe White chooses to pin us! We will break the pin.} 6...Be7 7.e3 {White supports d4. But now we can make use of that e4 square we control so well.} 7...Ne4 8.Nxe4 {White exchanges off the dominating knight, and we will recapture with the pawn, gaining space and attacking the knight.} 8...fxe4 9.Bxe7 {White takes our bishop, as their bishop on g5 was attacked twice. Our queen will recapture.} 9...Qxe7 10.Nd2 {White retreats, applying pressure on e4. We will castle, with our rook taking aim at f2!} 10...O-O 11.Be2 {White needs to castle quickly, so Be2 is necessary!} 11...d5 {Here we can break in the center, supporting our advanced e4 pawn, while giving our knight a better square to develop to on d7! The half-open f-file gives us the chance of a rook lift in the future, with kingside attacking chances!} *

[Event "Chapter 5: White Plays d4 & c4 / Study 2: The d4 & c4 Sidelines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/6cb135aa-4ae5-4c8a-b156-ef8211cd1138/studies/6b14cdbe-48eb-4422-aa4e-08bd318a5673/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the final study of this chapter! You're becoming quite the e6/b6 expert, aren't you? Well in this chapter we'll continue analyzing what happens after white opens with d4 and c4, but what if they prevent our idea of Bb4 pinning the knight with 4. a3? Here you'll find how to respond! Let's first start with our fianchetto.} 1...b6 2.c4 {White plays c4, controlling d5 from the side. We will develop with ...Bb7 and gain control of the long diagonal.} 2...Bb7 3.Nc3 {White's knight comes out, supporting central squares. With ...e6, we prepare to develop our dark-squared bishop. However...} 3...e6 4.a3 {White prevents Bb4 by preemptively playing a3! We will just continue gaining control of e4. Here you'll be introduced to potential responses White might play.} 4...f5 5.Nf3 {With Nf3 White continues normally, with development and central control. We will jump to f6, controlling e4 and d5 secondarily. After this White has four main options.} 5...Nf6 6.g3 {g3 prepares White's fianchetto. White wishes to contest the good h1-a8 diagonal. We will make use of the well-controlled e4 square!} 6...Ne4 7.Bg2 {White achieves their desired setup. However, we will now damage the pawns! Our control of e4 yielded us the secondary prize of better pawn structure.} 7...Nxc3 8.bxc3 {White recaptures the only way possible, and we will develop our dark-squared bishop.} 8...Be7 9.O-O {White secures the king.} 9...O-O {And so do we. We'll develop the knight next, and the dark-squared bishop will sit nicely on f6, controlling the diagonal if White decides to push d5. You'll see we still have to develop our knight on b8. This can be done via a6-c5, should White push forward with d5. If White doesn't push d5, we'll simply jump to c6.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: White Plays The Catalan / Study 1: Fighting The Catalan / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/d611379d-3828-4345-99a6-e4cf0a192b46/studies/f0f3cccd-5c29-4e5e-8ff6-de8ecd7fed04/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the chapter on countering the Catalan. In this study, you'll learn some strategies when white chooses to go on the h1-a8 diagonal after 1.d4 b6 2.c4 Bb7 3.Nf3 e6 and either g3 or a3 on move 4. First, let's prepare the fianchetto.} 1...b6 2.c4 {White plays c4, controlling the central d5 square from the side. d5 is an important square in these variations, so pay close attention to the play surrounding it. Let's fianchetto the bishop with an eye on g2.} 2...Bb7 3.Nf3 {White brings the knight out, blocking our bishop's diagonal. Taking the knight with 3...Bxf3 makes little sense here, as White could then capture towards the center with 4. gxf3, followed by playing e4, and they'd have a powerful center. Let's continue with e6, with the idea of playing Bb4 should White choose to bring a knight to c3.} 3...e6 4.g3 {White chooses to enter the h1-a8 diagonal. Here we can in reply push f5 and control e4 further.} 4...f5 5.Bg2 {White gets to the desired diagonal. And we continue development with an eye to e4.} 5...Nf6 6.O-O {White castles. And here very important: anticipating that White may end up going d5, we can choose to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop, which in turn will control many dark squares running along the a1-h8 diagonal. Thus we continue with ...g6.} 6...g6 7.Nc3 {White can continue with normal development by playing their knight to c3. We will occupy the long diagonal.} 7...Bg7 8.Bg5 {This pins our knight to the queen. Let's just castle and give our knight another defender.} 8...O-O 9.d5 {This move is almost always possible. Let's just jump to a6, looking at the c5 square.} 9...Na6 10.e4 {Should White choose to play e4 because our knight on f6 is still pinned... We should immediately pose a question to the bishop in order to break the pin.} 10...h6 {...h6 asking the bishop a question.} 11.Bxf6 {White trades. And we recapture with a view towards excellent control of the h8-a1 diagonal.} 11...Qxf6 12.e5 {e5, closing down the diagonal and attacking the queen seems reasonable for White. But we stay near the action of the kingside on f7.} 12...Qf7 13.Re1 {White adds a defender to e5. We add a potential attacker by bringing a rook to e8, as after exd5 we would open up an attack on the e5-pawn.} 13...Rae8 14.Qd2 {White connects rooks, but in the resulting position we have ideas of knight to c5 and then to e4, where we have good control.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: White Plays The Catalan / Study 1: Fighting The Catalan / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/d611379d-3828-4345-99a6-e4cf0a192b46/studies/f0f3cccd-5c29-4e5e-8ff6-de8ecd7fed04/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the chapter on countering the Catalan. In this study, you'll learn some strategies when white chooses to go on the h1-a8 diagonal after 1.d4 b6 2.c4 Bb7 3.Nf3 e6 and either g3 or a3 on move 4. First, let's prepare the fianchetto.} 1...b6 2.c4 {White plays c4, controlling the central d5 square from the side. d5 is an important square in these variations, so pay close attention to the play surrounding it. Let's fianchetto the bishop with an eye on g2.} 2...Bb7 3.Nf3 {White brings the knight out, blocking our bishop's diagonal. Taking the knight with 3...Bxf3 makes little sense here, as White could then capture towards the center with 4. gxf3, followed by playing e4, and they'd have a powerful center. Let's continue with e6, with the idea of playing Bb4 should White choose to bring a knight to c3.} 3...e6 4.g3 {White chooses to enter the h1-a8 diagonal. Here we can in reply push f5 and control e4 further.} 4...f5 5.Bg2 {White gets to the desired diagonal. And we continue development with an eye to e4.} 5...Nf6 6.O-O {White castles. And here very important: anticipating that White may end up going d5, we can choose to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop, which in turn will control many dark squares running along the a1-h8 diagonal. Thus we continue with ...g6.} 6...g6 7.d5 {White can also decide to push now. We can now enter the diagonal weakened by the d5 push.} 7...Bg7 8.dxe6 {White can opt for the very natural capture here. We will recapture.} 8...dxe6 9.Qxd8+ {Remember that trades benefit the side with less space. We recapture.} 9...Kxd8 10.Rd1+ {Not all checks are scary, you know. Sidestepping is a valid continuation. Furthermore, we defend our bishop against any discoveries from the knight.} 10...Kc8 11.Nc3 {White continues with regular development. Let's bring a rook in to defend our weakest link in the position.} 11...Re8 12.Bf4 {White develops looking at c7 which is defended by our king. We continue with development. We realize that if White plays Nb5 the d7-knight supports an e5 pawn push, blocking the f4-bishop.} 12...Nbd7 13.Nb5 {White converges on c7.} 13...e5 {But we're in time to block off the attacker. We'll continue by attacking the knight on b5 and improving our knight via c5.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: White Plays The Catalan / Study 1: Fighting The Catalan / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/d611379d-3828-4345-99a6-e4cf0a192b46/studies/f0f3cccd-5c29-4e5e-8ff6-de8ecd7fed04/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the chapter on countering the Catalan. In this study, you'll learn some strategies when white chooses to go on the h1-a8 diagonal after 1.d4 b6 2.c4 Bb7 3.Nf3 e6 and either g3 or a3 on move 4. First, let's prepare the fianchetto.} 1...b6 2.c4 {White plays c4, controlling the central d5 square from the side. d5 is an important square in these variations, so pay close attention to the play surrounding it. Let's fianchetto the bishop with an eye on g2.} 2...Bb7 3.Nf3 {White brings the knight out, blocking our bishop's diagonal. Taking the knight with 3...Bxf3 makes little sense here, as White could then capture towards the center with 4. gxf3, followed by playing e4, and they'd have a powerful center. Let's continue with e6, with the idea of playing Bb4 should White choose to bring a knight to c3.} 3...e6 4.g3 {White chooses to enter the h1-a8 diagonal. Here we can in reply push f5 and control e4 further.} 4...f5 5.Bg2 {White gets to the desired diagonal. And we continue development with an eye to e4.} 5...Nf6 6.O-O {White castles. And here very important: anticipating that White may end up going d5, we can choose to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop, which in turn will control many dark squares running along the a1-h8 diagonal. Thus we continue with ...g6.} 6...g6 7.Nc3 {White can continue with normal development by playing their knight to c3. We will occupy the long diagonal.} 7...Bg7 8.d5 {This move gains space for White, and blocks off our bishop on b7. In reply we will take advantage of the recently made available c5 square by playing ...Na6.} 8...Na6 9.Nd4 {Nd4 converges on our pawn. We will castle, sacrificing a pawn!} 9...O-O 10.dxe6 {White opens up the bishop and takes a pawn. First we will capture that bishop.} 10...Bxg2 11.Kxg2 {The king recaptures. Now we go to c5, applying pressure to the e6-pawn.} 11...Nc5 12.exd7 {Should White choose to grab a pawn...} 12...Qxd7 {We recapture with the queen, connecting rooks and completing our development. We have pressure on the knight and on the open e and d files, so getting some compensation for that pawn should be no problem at all.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: White Plays The Catalan / Study 1: Fighting The Catalan / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/d611379d-3828-4345-99a6-e4cf0a192b46/studies/f0f3cccd-5c29-4e5e-8ff6-de8ecd7fed04/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the chapter on countering the Catalan. In this study, you'll learn some strategies when white chooses to go on the h1-a8 diagonal after 1.d4 b6 2.c4 Bb7 3.Nf3 e6 and either g3 or a3 on move 4. First, let's prepare the fianchetto.} 1...b6 2.c4 {White plays c4, controlling the central d5 square from the side. d5 is an important square in these variations, so pay close attention to the play surrounding it. Let's fianchetto the bishop with an eye on g2.} 2...Bb7 3.Nf3 {White brings the knight out, blocking our bishop's diagonal. Taking the knight with 3...Bxf3 makes little sense here, as White could then capture towards the center with 4. gxf3, followed by playing e4, and they'd have a powerful center. Let's continue with e6, with the idea of playing Bb4 should White choose to bring a knight to c3.} 3...e6 4.a3 {This move order prevents us from developing to b4. After 4.a3 we can respond with f5 to control the e4 square, as we have already done throughout this course.} 4...f5 5.g3 {White plays g3 to enter the diagonal themselves. But we develop, further controlling e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg2 {After Bg2 entering the coveted diagonal, I suggest a double bishop fianchetto system. We will play g6.} 6...g6 7.Nc3 {White develops normally. And we fulfill our idea of bringing the bishop to g7.} 7...Bg7 8.O-O {White castles. Black castles as well.} 8...O-O 9.d5 {And now the advance to d5 is played. After this point you'll notice that the c5 square has been given up by White. So let's develop a piece with the idea of jumping to c5 at some point.} 9...Na6 10.Bf4 {White develops yet another piece, this time applying pressure on c7. But we can opt for using the recently available c5 square.} 10...Nc5 11.Rc1 {After the rook joins a more active file.} 11...Nfe4 {We can play our f-knight to e4, making use of that central square and proposing a trade of knights. With less space on the board, achieving trades is a good thing.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: White Plays The Catalan / Study 1: Fighting The Catalan / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/d611379d-3828-4345-99a6-e4cf0a192b46/studies/f0f3cccd-5c29-4e5e-8ff6-de8ecd7fed04/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the chapter on countering the Catalan. In this study, you'll learn some strategies when white chooses to go on the h1-a8 diagonal after 1.d4 b6 2.c4 Bb7 3.Nf3 e6 and either g3 or a3 on move 4. First, let's prepare the fianchetto.} 1...b6 2.c4 {White plays c4, controlling the central d5 square from the side. d5 is an important square in these variations, so pay close attention to the play surrounding it. Let's fianchetto the bishop with an eye on g2.} 2...Bb7 3.Nf3 {White brings the knight out, blocking our bishop's diagonal. Taking the knight with 3...Bxf3 makes little sense here, as White could then capture towards the center with 4. gxf3, followed by playing e4, and they'd have a powerful center. Let's continue with e6, with the idea of playing Bb4 should White choose to bring a knight to c3.} 3...e6 4.g3 {White chooses to enter the h1-a8 diagonal. Here we can in reply push f5 and control e4 further.} 4...f5 5.Bg2 {White gets to the desired diagonal. And we continue development with an eye to e4.} 5...Nf6 6.O-O {White castles. And here very important: anticipating that White may end up going d5, we can choose to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop, which in turn will control many dark squares running along the a1-h8 diagonal. Thus we continue with ...g6.} 6...g6 7.Nc3 {White can continue with normal development by playing their knight to c3. We will occupy the long diagonal.} 7...Bg7 8.d5 {This move gains space for White, and blocks off our bishop on b7. In reply we will take advantage of the recently made available c5 square by playing ...Na6.} 8...Na6 9.Bf4 {Bf4 develops the bishop for White. We can calmly castle.} 9...O-O 10.Rc1 {White puts their rook somewhere more useful.} 10...Qc8 {And we move the queen to the side where it defends our bishop and lends some support to our pawn chain. Play may continue with ideas of Re8, going for an e5 push when it is sufficiently prepared, or by integrating the knight through c5 and e4.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: White Plays The Catalan / Study 1: Fighting The Catalan / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/d611379d-3828-4345-99a6-e4cf0a192b46/studies/f0f3cccd-5c29-4e5e-8ff6-de8ecd7fed04/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the chapter on countering the Catalan. In this study, you'll learn some strategies when white chooses to go on the h1-a8 diagonal after 1.d4 b6 2.c4 Bb7 3.Nf3 e6 and either g3 or a3 on move 4. First, let's prepare the fianchetto.} 1...b6 2.c4 {White plays c4, controlling the central d5 square from the side. d5 is an important square in these variations, so pay close attention to the play surrounding it. Let's fianchetto the bishop with an eye on g2.} 2...Bb7 3.Nf3 {White brings the knight out, blocking our bishop's diagonal. Taking the knight with 3...Bxf3 makes little sense here, as White could then capture towards the center with 4. gxf3, followed by playing e4, and they'd have a powerful center. Let's continue with e6, with the idea of playing Bb4 should White choose to bring a knight to c3.} 3...e6 4.g3 {White chooses to enter the h1-a8 diagonal. Here we can in reply push f5 and control e4 further.} 4...f5 5.Bg2 {White gets to the desired diagonal. And we continue development with an eye to e4.} 5...Nf6 6.O-O {White castles. And here very important: anticipating that White may end up going d5, we can choose to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop, which in turn will control many dark squares running along the a1-h8 diagonal. Thus we continue with ...g6.} 6...g6 7.Nc3 {White can continue with normal development by playing their knight to c3. We will occupy the long diagonal.} 7...Bg7 8.d5 {This move gains space for White, and blocks off our bishop on b7. In reply we will take advantage of the recently made available c5 square by playing ...Na6.} 8...Na6 9.Bg5 {Bg5 pins us. Don't move the knight! We will castle.} 9...O-O 10.e4 {Should White choose to play e4 because our knight on f6 is still pinned... We should immediately pose a question to the bishop in order to break the pin.} 10...h6 {...h6 asking the bishop a question.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: White Plays The Catalan / Study 1: Fighting The Catalan / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/d611379d-3828-4345-99a6-e4cf0a192b46/studies/f0f3cccd-5c29-4e5e-8ff6-de8ecd7fed04/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the chapter on countering the Catalan. In this study, you'll learn some strategies when white chooses to go on the h1-a8 diagonal after 1.d4 b6 2.c4 Bb7 3.Nf3 e6 and either g3 or a3 on move 4. First, let's prepare the fianchetto.} 1...b6 2.c4 {White plays c4, controlling the central d5 square from the side. d5 is an important square in these variations, so pay close attention to the play surrounding it. Let's fianchetto the bishop with an eye on g2.} 2...Bb7 3.Nf3 {White brings the knight out, blocking our bishop's diagonal. Taking the knight with 3...Bxf3 makes little sense here, as White could then capture towards the center with 4. gxf3, followed by playing e4, and they'd have a powerful center. Let's continue with e6, with the idea of playing Bb4 should White choose to bring a knight to c3.} 3...e6 4.g3 {White chooses to enter the h1-a8 diagonal. Here we can in reply push f5 and control e4 further.} 4...f5 5.Bg2 {White gets to the desired diagonal. And we continue development with an eye to e4.} 5...Nf6 6.O-O {White castles. And here very important: anticipating that White may end up going d5, we can choose to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop, which in turn will control many dark squares running along the a1-h8 diagonal. Thus we continue with ...g6.} 6...g6 7.Nc3 {White can continue with normal development by playing their knight to c3. We will occupy the long diagonal.} 7...Bg7 8.Bf4 {White continues developing their forces. In reply we can simply castle.} 8...O-O 9.Qd2 {White forms the battery with the queen and bishop.} 9...d6 {And we play d6, controlling e5. In the resulting position we'll have ideas of trading the c3-knight for our knight after jumping to e4, developing the knight to d7 and our queen to e7.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: White Plays The Catalan / Study 1: Fighting The Catalan / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/d611379d-3828-4345-99a6-e4cf0a192b46/studies/f0f3cccd-5c29-4e5e-8ff6-de8ecd7fed04/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the chapter on countering the Catalan. In this study, you'll learn some strategies when white chooses to go on the h1-a8 diagonal after 1.d4 b6 2.c4 Bb7 3.Nf3 e6 and either g3 or a3 on move 4. First, let's prepare the fianchetto.} 1...b6 2.c4 {White plays c4, controlling the central d5 square from the side. d5 is an important square in these variations, so pay close attention to the play surrounding it. Let's fianchetto the bishop with an eye on g2.} 2...Bb7 3.Nf3 {White brings the knight out, blocking our bishop's diagonal. Taking the knight with 3...Bxf3 makes little sense here, as White could then capture towards the center with 4. gxf3, followed by playing e4, and they'd have a powerful center. Let's continue with e6, with the idea of playing Bb4 should White choose to bring a knight to c3.} 3...e6 4.g3 {White chooses to enter the h1-a8 diagonal. Here we can in reply push f5 and control e4 further.} 4...f5 5.Bg2 {White gets to the desired diagonal. And we continue development with an eye to e4.} 5...Nf6 6.O-O {White castles. And here very important: anticipating that White may end up going d5, we can choose to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop, which in turn will control many dark squares running along the a1-h8 diagonal. Thus we continue with ...g6.} 6...g6 7.Nc3 {White can continue with normal development by playing their knight to c3. We will occupy the long diagonal.} 7...Bg7 8.Bg5 {This pins our knight to the queen. Let's just castle and give our knight another defender.} 8...O-O 9.Qd2 {White forms a bishop and queen battery, maybe with the idea of trading our good bishop on g7 via Bh6. Let's get the queen out of the pin.} 9...Qe8 10.Rad1 {White adds another piece to the d-file. Now we can jump to e4, attacking the queen and thus we'll probably achieve a trade, which is good when we have less space in a position.} 10...Ne4 11.Nxe4 {Removing the pressure.} 11...Bxe4 {And we just recapture with our bishop. Very natural developing moves will follow. The move ...d6 will be played to cover some central squares. We can expect Bh6 from White, to trade off the dark-squared bishops. In that case, we can simply trade and bring our queen to e7.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Anti-London / Study 1: A System Against the London / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/d204f5db-9c63-4992-ab86-0face6714b40/studies/c0f83b22-f642-4c87-8969-dc1398b95800/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the chapter on the London. What to do when white goes for the very common and solid London setup? Well, we have our repertoire of strategies, going for a fianchetto of our bishop, controlling e4 via f5 and proposing an early trade on d6! Let's prepare to fianchetto as normal.} 1...b6 2.Bf4 {And now this is the clear indication that White will go for the London. We will simply fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nf3 {White develops the knight in typical London fashion. We will open up our bishop and control some central light squares.} 3...e6 {Our moves remain the same.} 4.e3 {White continues and supports d4 and f4. We will play f5, controlling e4 from the side.} 4...f5 {And we play f5, controlling e4. As you can see, White might alter the move order, but our moves remain the same.} 5.Bd3 {White develops, looking at the e4 square. We develop our knight, fighting for more control of e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Nbd2 {White develops to d2, as is done in the London. White plans to play a pawn to c3. Our idea is to immediately challenge the dark-squared bishop with Bd6. This is one of White's best pieces. If we trade it, there will be less pressure on our position.} 6...Bd6 7.Bxd6 {This is another rational response. Maybe White thinks that doubling our pawns is bad. On the contrary, another central pawn can be very useful! We recapture. Our extra d-pawn helps control the e5 square! White won't get their usual Ne5 play!} 7...cxd6 8.O-O {White castles... And so do we.} 8...O-O 9.c3 {White achieves their London structure. We develop the knight to c6. It often travels to e7.} 9...Nc6 10.Re1 {White's idea here is to push e4. And now we allow White to play e4 if and only if they want massive trades in the center!} 10...Ne7 11.e4 {So after e4... We take.} 11...fxe4 12.Nxe4 {They recapture. But we can trade both minor pieces off.} 12...Nxe4 13.Bxe4 {They recapture. And we will exchange bishops.} 13...Bxe4 14.Rxe4 {After the dust has settled...} 14...Qc7 {It's hard to see how White's going to win, and our rook is placed on the semi-open f-file!} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Anti-London / Study 1: A System Against the London / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/d204f5db-9c63-4992-ab86-0face6714b40/studies/c0f83b22-f642-4c87-8969-dc1398b95800/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the chapter on the London. What to do when white goes for the very common and solid London setup? Well, we have our repertoire of strategies, going for a fianchetto of our bishop, controlling e4 via f5 and proposing an early trade on d6! Let's prepare to fianchetto as normal.} 1...b6 2.Bf4 {And now this is the clear indication that White will go for the London. We will simply fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nf3 {White develops the knight in typical London fashion. We will open up our bishop and control some central light squares.} 3...e6 {Our moves remain the same.} 4.e3 {White continues and supports d4 and f4. We will play f5, controlling e4 from the side.} 4...f5 {And we play f5, controlling e4. As you can see, White might alter the move order, but our moves remain the same.} 5.Bd3 {White develops, looking at the e4 square. We develop our knight, fighting for more control of e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Nbd2 {White develops to d2, as is done in the London. White plans to play a pawn to c3. Our idea is to immediately challenge the dark-squared bishop with Bd6. This is one of White's best pieces. If we trade it, there will be less pressure on our position.} 6...Bd6 7.Ne5 {White could stay true to London style of play, and thus they jump the knight to e5. Let's just castle. The knight might look good on e5, but it isn't harming us in any way.} 7...O-O 8.c3 {White achieves their London pawn structure. We will propose a knight trade with ...Nc6. White probably won't take.} 8...Nc6 9.Ndf3 {Instead, they often support their knight with the other knight. But we're maneuvering to d5 to harass the bishop!} 9...Ne7 10.Qe2 {For example, White may well develop their queen... We will go after the bishop pair.} 10...Ned5 11.Bg3 {And if the bishop retreats...} 11...Nh5 {We insist on getting that bishop! Maybe White should have just traded on d6 earlier!} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Anti-London / Study 1: A System Against the London / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/d204f5db-9c63-4992-ab86-0face6714b40/studies/c0f83b22-f642-4c87-8969-dc1398b95800/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the chapter on the London. What to do when white goes for the very common and solid London setup? Well, we have our repertoire of strategies, going for a fianchetto of our bishop, controlling e4 via f5 and proposing an early trade on d6! Let's prepare to fianchetto as normal.} 1...b6 2.Bf4 {And now this is the clear indication that White will go for the London. We will simply fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nf3 {White develops the knight in typical London fashion. We will open up our bishop and control some central light squares.} 3...e6 {Our moves remain the same.} 4.e3 {White continues and supports d4 and f4. We will play f5, controlling e4 from the side.} 4...f5 {And we play f5, controlling e4. As you can see, White might alter the move order, but our moves remain the same.} 5.Bd3 {White develops, looking at the e4 square. We develop our knight, fighting for more control of e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Nbd2 {White develops to d2, as is done in the London. White plans to play a pawn to c3. Our idea is to immediately challenge the dark-squared bishop with Bd6. This is one of White's best pieces. If we trade it, there will be less pressure on our position.} 6...Bd6 7.c3 {This is one of White's rarer responses. It simply chooses to do nothing about the tension between the bishops. White is figuring that moving their bishop loses tempo. We will swap on f4 and double White's pawns.} 7...Bxf4 8.exf4 {White recaptures. We will castle.} 8...O-O 9.O-O {And so does White. We will develop our knight.} 9...Nc6 10.Ne5 {Ne5 is not a surprise at all. But we can jump into the center too and attack f4.} 10...Nd5 11.g3 {Suppose White guards f4 by playing g3... We can trade and tempt White to undouble their pawns.} 11...Nxe5 12.fxe5 {Once they do this... Qg5 threatens f4, which is better for Black.} 12...Qg5 13.Nf3 {And if White doesn't play f4 themselves...} 13...Qh5 {Our queen stays lurking in the kingside, where it threatens to continue the assault on White's position. We have an excellent position, as the light squares in front of the White king are weak.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Anti-London / Study 1: A System Against the London / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/d204f5db-9c63-4992-ab86-0face6714b40/studies/c0f83b22-f642-4c87-8969-dc1398b95800/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the chapter on the London. What to do when white goes for the very common and solid London setup? Well, we have our repertoire of strategies, going for a fianchetto of our bishop, controlling e4 via f5 and proposing an early trade on d6! Let's prepare to fianchetto as normal.} 1...b6 2.Bf4 {And now this is the clear indication that White will go for the London. We will simply fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nf3 {White develops the knight in typical London fashion. We will open up our bishop and control some central light squares.} 3...e6 {Our moves remain the same.} 4.e3 {White continues and supports d4 and f4. We will play f5, controlling e4 from the side.} 4...f5 {And we play f5, controlling e4. As you can see, White might alter the move order, but our moves remain the same.} 5.Bd3 {White develops, looking at the e4 square. We develop our knight, fighting for more control of e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Nbd2 {White develops to d2, as is done in the London. White plans to play a pawn to c3. Our idea is to immediately challenge the dark-squared bishop with Bd6. This is one of White's best pieces. If we trade it, there will be less pressure on our position.} 6...Bd6 7.Bg3 {This is White's most popular response. It's actually a knee-jerk reaction from many London players. They don't tend to trade, but many just go back to g3 so they can trade on their terms. We'll simply castle.} 7...O-O 8.c3 {If they play a useful waiting move like c3... Exchanging is actually quite safe.} 8...Bxg3 9.hxg3 {Because even though the rook gets a semi-open file... If we can control the queen's entry square, we have a safe position.} 9...Qe8 10.Qe2 {That's why if White attemps to play e4 to get some more squares for their pieces... We put our bishop on e4. A trade on this square will close the position in our favor, and White's queen will be limited to the light squares.} 10...Be4 11.Bxe4 {So if White trades bishops... We recapture with the pawn.} 11...fxe4 12.Ng5 {The knight jumps forward and attacks e4.} 12...d5 {We can just push and consolidate our structure, and the e-pawn is defended by the queen. White's knight is a bit stranded on the g-file, and they'll have to think of ways to relocate it.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Anti-London / Study 1: A System Against the London / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/d204f5db-9c63-4992-ab86-0face6714b40/studies/c0f83b22-f642-4c87-8969-dc1398b95800/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the chapter on the London. What to do when white goes for the very common and solid London setup? Well, we have our repertoire of strategies, going for a fianchetto of our bishop, controlling e4 via f5 and proposing an early trade on d6! Let's prepare to fianchetto as normal.} 1...b6 2.Bf4 {And now this is the clear indication that White will go for the London. We will simply fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nf3 {White develops the knight in typical London fashion. We will open up our bishop and control some central light squares.} 3...e6 {Our moves remain the same.} 4.e3 {White continues and supports d4 and f4. We will play f5, controlling e4 from the side.} 4...f5 {And we play f5, controlling e4. As you can see, White might alter the move order, but our moves remain the same.} 5.Bd3 {White develops, looking at the e4 square. We develop our knight, fighting for more control of e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Nbd2 {White develops to d2, as is done in the London. White plans to play a pawn to c3. Our idea is to immediately challenge the dark-squared bishop with Bd6. This is one of White's best pieces. If we trade it, there will be less pressure on our position.} 6...Bd6 7.Bg5 {White chooses to reject the trade and they pin us instead. We unpin with ...Be7. A future Ne4 will revive the idea of trading.} 7...Be7 8.O-O {White castles. We insist on trying to trade pieces off.} 8...Ne4 9.Bf4 {Maybe White elects to keep the bishop on. We will go ahead and castle.} 9...O-O 10.c3 {White gets the traditional triangle pawn structure of the London.} 10...d5 {And we cement our knight on e4. We've also got ideas of g5 at some point!} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Anti-London / Study 1: A System Against the London / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/d204f5db-9c63-4992-ab86-0face6714b40/studies/c0f83b22-f642-4c87-8969-dc1398b95800/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the chapter on the London. What to do when white goes for the very common and solid London setup? Well, we have our repertoire of strategies, going for a fianchetto of our bishop, controlling e4 via f5 and proposing an early trade on d6! Let's prepare to fianchetto as normal.} 1...b6 2.Bf4 {And now this is the clear indication that White will go for the London. We will simply fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nf3 {White develops the knight in typical London fashion. We will open up our bishop and control some central light squares.} 3...e6 {Our moves remain the same.} 4.e3 {White continues and supports d4 and f4. We will play f5, controlling e4 from the side.} 4...f5 {And we play f5, controlling e4. As you can see, White might alter the move order, but our moves remain the same.} 5.Bd3 {White develops, looking at the e4 square. We develop our knight, fighting for more control of e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Nbd2 {White develops to d2, as is done in the London. White plans to play a pawn to c3. Our idea is to immediately challenge the dark-squared bishop with Bd6. This is one of White's best pieces. If we trade it, there will be less pressure on our position.} 6...Bd6 7.Bg5 {White chooses to reject the trade and they pin us instead. We unpin with ...Be7. A future Ne4 will revive the idea of trading.} 7...Be7 8.O-O {White castles. We insist on trying to trade pieces off.} 8...Ne4 9.Bxe7 {White could accept the trade. We recapture.} 9...Qxe7 10.Ne5 {Ne5 is to be expected in the London. Simply react to it normally. Castling is always useful.} 10...O-O 11.f3 {The position of our knight is very strong on e4. That's why White may try to kick it out. We go back, but now we're threatening to expel the strong e5-knight.} 11...Nf6 12.Qe2 {White connects their rooks. We will play ...d6 and kick the knight out.} 12...d6 13.Nec4 {It goes to the only square available. Now g6 supports the f-pawn in case of e4.} 13...g6 14.e4 {So after White's break on e4, attacking our f-pawn, we now have a defender in the form of the g6 pawn.} 14...Nh5 {And additionally we can threaten to fork White! We're basically in the middle game, and White hasn't achieved any advantage!} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Anti-London / Study 1: A System Against the London / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/d204f5db-9c63-4992-ab86-0face6714b40/studies/c0f83b22-f642-4c87-8969-dc1398b95800/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the chapter on the London. What to do when white goes for the very common and solid London setup? Well, we have our repertoire of strategies, going for a fianchetto of our bishop, controlling e4 via f5 and proposing an early trade on d6! Let's prepare to fianchetto as normal.} 1...b6 2.Bf4 {And now this is the clear indication that White will go for the London. We will simply fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.e3 {Should White go e3 first, do not dismay. It's just a slightly different move order. Our third move is still e6.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {White develops to f3 in true London-style.} 4...f5 {And we play f5, controlling e4. As you can see, White might alter the move order, but our moves remain the same.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Anti-London / Study 1: A System Against the London / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/d204f5db-9c63-4992-ab86-0face6714b40/studies/c0f83b22-f642-4c87-8969-dc1398b95800/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the chapter on the London. What to do when white goes for the very common and solid London setup? Well, we have our repertoire of strategies, going for a fianchetto of our bishop, controlling e4 via f5 and proposing an early trade on d6! Let's prepare to fianchetto as normal.} 1...b6 2.Nf3 {Alternatively, White could bring out the knight first. In which case, they could still be going for a London setup, as after we develop, they'd play Bf4. We fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Bf4 {White develops the knight in typical London fashion. We will open up our bishop and control some central light squares.} 3...e6 {Our moves remain the same.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: Beating The English / Study 1: White's Kingside Fianchetto English / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/9fe299ec-9537-4984-924f-cdf6ba831914/studies/2b920e25-18a5-4372-b923-7a5d6340240b/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to the chapter on beating the English. In this study, we'll begin by looking at how to play against White's very common setup fianchettoing the light-squared bishop in the English structure. We'll also introduce the Black player to some of the intricacies of playing against the English. We begin our development with b6, like in the previous d4 chapters.} 1...b6 2.Nc3 {White develops a knight, which is poised to control d5 as well. We now enter the diagonal with our fianchettoed bishop.} 2...Bb7 3.Nf3 {White develops the knight where it controls the most central squares. Additionally, they block off the diagonal from our bishop. In reply, we will play e6, preparing to strike in the central light squares and opening the diagonal of our dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.g3 {White can decide they want to join the powerful h1-a8 diagonal as well, since right now we occupy it unopposed. In this course we'll recommend ...Bb4, with the idea of damaging White's pawns, and removing a piece that controls d5 and e4.} 4...Bb4 5.Bg2 {If White develops in this standard way, we feel free to capture on c3.} 5...Bxc3 6.bxc3 {White recaptures towards the center. We will continue with normal development, placing our knight on e7.} 6...Ne7 7.O-O {White castles. And so do we.} 7...O-O 8.d3 {d3 defends c4. You'll notice no pawn can now defend the backwards c3 pawn. We play the sound d6, placing our pawns on the dark-square complex since we have parted with our dark-square bishop.} 8...d6 9.e4 {White pushes and gains space with e4. d6 also freed a square for our knight to develop to, and we'll place our knight on d7.} 9...Nd7 10.Re1 {White places the rook on a more active square. And yet another pawn in the dark-square complex. A sound strategy like Capablanca and Nimzowitsch used to employ.} 10...e5 11.a4 {White attempts to gain space on the queenside.} 11...a5 {You know the drill! Stop White from gaining space on the queenside and place another pawn on a dark square. Seems like win-win for us. We might expect moves like d4 and Nh4 from White. In that case, we can counter-attack with ...f5!} *

[Event "Chapter 8: Beating The English / Study 2: The 2 Knights English / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/9fe299ec-9537-4984-924f-cdf6ba831914/studies/5900e240-30f7-440d-94cf-7edb81b13344/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to the final study of this chapter! You'll learn how to combat a very common setup White can go for in the English. After 1.c4 b6 2.Nc3 Bb7 3.e4 e6 4.Nf3 Bb4 - you can refer to this as the Two Knights in the English by White. We will prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 1...b6 2.Nc3 {White develops, controlling central squares. We'll get our bishop onto the effective a8-h1 diagonal.} 2...Bb7 3.e4 {White's knight sufficiently defends e4, so White stakes a claim to the center. Let's prepare to bring our bishop out with ...e6.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {White develops, controlling central squares. After our reply of ...Bb4, we threaten to take on c3, double the pawns, and win e4 in the process!} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {White can continue with this move, which defends the e4-pawn after a potential bishop capture on c3. We'll just continue with normal development, and bring the knight to e7.} 5...Ne7 6.O-O {White gets their king into safety. We'll do likewise.} 6...O-O 7.Bc2 {White frees the way for the d-pawn. Here we can strike in the center! If exf5, our bishop will look at f3 and our rook will exert pressure down the f-file.} 7...f5 8.Qe2 {White can ignore this push and simply develop their queen. We'll develop to c6.} 8...Nbc6 9.d4 {White pushes d4, gaining space! We capture on c3, damaging the pawns, and undermining e4.} 9...Bxc3 10.bxc3 {White recaptures towards the center. With ...Na5, we open up our bishop, apply pressure on c4 with the knight, and pressure on e4 with the bishop.} 10...Na5 11.d5 {White gains space. We'll strike at c4 with ...Ba6.} 11...Ba6 12.Bd3 {White defends the c4-pawn. We'll capture on d5.} 12...exd5 13.exd5 {White recaptures, opening a line to f5. At the moment, though, they are busy defending c4. We can bring our knight to a more active square, covering a potential e5 invasion from the f3-knight.} 13...Ng6 14.Re1 {White doubles major pieces on the e-file. But we can propose a trade by gaining a tempo on the queen. This would be advantageous as c4 is weak, and becomes weaker the more pieces we can exchange.} 14...Re8 15.Qc2 {White chooses to keep the queen on the board. We will still swap rooks, though.} 15...Rxe1+ 16.Nxe1 {White recaptures the only way possible. We gain control of the e-file with tempo, hitting the knight on e1.} 16...Qe8 17.Bg5 {White develops a bishop and defends e1 with the rook. But now we can take on c4.} 17...Bxc4 18.Bxf5 {White can grab our f-pawn, but...} 18...Bxd5 {We can also gobble up the remains of their center! Our plan is to make room for our rook with ...Qf7, after which we can retreat the bishop to b7 or improve our knight via c4.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: Beating The English / Study 2: The 2 Knights English / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/9fe299ec-9537-4984-924f-cdf6ba831914/studies/5900e240-30f7-440d-94cf-7edb81b13344/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to the final study of this chapter! You'll learn how to combat a very common setup White can go for in the English. After 1.c4 b6 2.Nc3 Bb7 3.e4 e6 4.Nf3 Bb4 - you can refer to this as the Two Knights in the English by White. We will prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 1...b6 2.Nc3 {White develops, controlling central squares. We'll get our bishop onto the effective a8-h1 diagonal.} 2...Bb7 3.e4 {White's knight sufficiently defends e4, so White stakes a claim to the center. Let's prepare to bring our bishop out with ...e6.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {White develops, controlling central squares. After our reply of ...Bb4, we threaten to take on c3, double the pawns, and win e4 in the process!} 4...Bb4 5.e5 {Finally, White can see they're under attack on e4 so they can push the pawn up one square, with the idea of gaining control of f6 and d6. We'll capture on f3, arguing that the removal of the supporting knight renders the white center over-extended.} 5...Bxf3 6.Qxf3 {White recaptures with a queen, gaining a tempo on our rook. But we block the diagonal and gain a tempo on e5 while also controlling d4.} 6...Nc6 7.Qg3 {White here defends e5 and attacks g7, but... We will play enterprisingly. We offer the g-pawn, with ideas of gaining tempi on the queen, and maybe even trapping her.} 7...Nge7 8.Qxg7 {White takes, and hits the rook. Here we defend the rook, and limit the mobility of the queen. We're also attacking e5 twice.} 8...Ng6 9.h4 {White can push the pawn, with the idea of removing the defender of h8. But we can recapture our pawn safely, because we have a nice trick up our sleeve.} 9...Ncxe5 10.h5 {White's original idea, but...} 10...Bf8 {We don't have to move the knight. The queen is trapped!} *

[Event "Chapter 8: Beating The English / Study 2: The 2 Knights English / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/9fe299ec-9537-4984-924f-cdf6ba831914/studies/5900e240-30f7-440d-94cf-7edb81b13344/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to the final study of this chapter! You'll learn how to combat a very common setup White can go for in the English. After 1.c4 b6 2.Nc3 Bb7 3.e4 e6 4.Nf3 Bb4 - you can refer to this as the Two Knights in the English by White. We will prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 1...b6 2.Nc3 {White develops, controlling central squares. We'll get our bishop onto the effective a8-h1 diagonal.} 2...Bb7 3.e4 {White's knight sufficiently defends e4, so White stakes a claim to the center. Let's prepare to bring our bishop out with ...e6.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {White develops, controlling central squares. After our reply of ...Bb4, we threaten to take on c3, double the pawns, and win e4 in the process!} 4...Bb4 5.Qc2 {White can also try Qc2, defending e4. Let's undermine the center in a provocative way, with ...f5!} 5...f5 6.exf5 {White captures. We'll take on f3, damaging the pawns.} 6...Bxf3 7.gxf3 {White recaptures. Quick development is key! We'll bring our queen's knight out.} 7...Nc6 8.fxe6 {White can greedily snatch another pawn... And we can develop yet another piece!} 8...Nf6 9.exd7+ {White can continue pawn-grabbing.} 9...Qxd7 {But then we end up with four developed pieces and a castled king, before White even finds a home for their king.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: Beating The English / Study 2: The 2 Knights English / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/9fe299ec-9537-4984-924f-cdf6ba831914/studies/5900e240-30f7-440d-94cf-7edb81b13344/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to the final study of this chapter! You'll learn how to combat a very common setup White can go for in the English. After 1.c4 b6 2.Nc3 Bb7 3.e4 e6 4.Nf3 Bb4 - you can refer to this as the Two Knights in the English by White. We will prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 1...b6 2.Nc3 {White develops, controlling central squares. We'll get our bishop onto the effective a8-h1 diagonal.} 2...Bb7 3.e4 {White's knight sufficiently defends e4, so White stakes a claim to the center. Let's prepare to bring our bishop out with ...e6.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {White develops, controlling central squares. After our reply of ...Bb4, we threaten to take on c3, double the pawns, and win e4 in the process!} 4...Bb4 5.d3 {White can also defend e4 with a pawn. Developing the knight with ...Ne7 is a good idea.} 5...Ne7 6.Be2 {White prepares to castle. We will castle.} 6...O-O 7.O-O {And so does White. Now we will play energetically, and strike out at the white center with ...f5.} 7...f5 8.e5 {White can try to avoid our aggressive intentions and keep lines closed by playing e5.} 8...Ng6 {But we can increase the pressure with our knight moving to g6.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: Beating The English / Study 2: The 2 Knights English / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/9fe299ec-9537-4984-924f-cdf6ba831914/studies/5900e240-30f7-440d-94cf-7edb81b13344/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to the final study of this chapter! You'll learn how to combat a very common setup White can go for in the English. After 1.c4 b6 2.Nc3 Bb7 3.e4 e6 4.Nf3 Bb4 - you can refer to this as the Two Knights in the English by White. We will prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 1...b6 2.Nc3 {White develops, controlling central squares. We'll get our bishop onto the effective a8-h1 diagonal.} 2...Bb7 3.e4 {White's knight sufficiently defends e4, so White stakes a claim to the center. Let's prepare to bring our bishop out with ...e6.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {White develops, controlling central squares. After our reply of ...Bb4, we threaten to take on c3, double the pawns, and win e4 in the process!} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {White can continue with this move, which defends the e4-pawn after a potential bishop capture on c3. We'll just continue with normal development, and bring the knight to e7.} 5...Ne7 6.a3 {If White attacks us on b4... We'll capture and damage their pawn structure.} 6...Bxc3 7.dxc3 {White recaptures away from the center, giving their queen some scope, and giving the c1-bishop a diagonal to work with. We'll castle into safety.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White castles.} 8...d6 {And we play d6, holding off on concrete central play, but placing a pawn on a dark-square since we have parted ways with our dark-squared bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: Beating The Reti / Study 1: The Reti with a Queenside Fianchetto / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/0b2870f6-73c4-467e-a424-25bf46685bc6/studies/2c58b574-7998-46f6-8809-5b79508abc25/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...b6 2.b3 {b3, with the idea of a fianchetto to control the a1-h8 diagonal. We fianchetto, stepping into the a8-h1 diagonal.} 2...Bb7 3.Bb2 {White controls their diagonal too. We play e6, getting ready for a potential d5 or f5 pawn push. Here White has many moves available to choose from. We'll go over the most popular ones. Don't move your bishop on f8 until you block off the white's bishop's access to g7!} 3...e6 4.g3 {This move indicates that White wants to go for the double fianchetto! A true hyper-modern way of playing! Here we can preface Nf6 blocking the diagonal with the pawn push ...f5. We are playing to control e4.} 4...f5 5.Bg2 {White will fianchetto. And we will play the knight to f6, blocking off the b2-bishop slightly.} 5...Nf6 6.O-O {White castles. We will develop the bishop and support the knight on f6.} 6...Be7 7.d3 {White might attempt to prepare an e4 push. Meanwhile, we're going to bring our king into safety.} 7...O-O 8.Nbd2 {White develops another piece, again supporting the central break with a future e4. We can expand on the queenside!} 8...a5 9.Re1 {Again with the idea of supporting e2-e4. We can develop our knight without blocking off our bishop.} 9...Na6 10.e4 {White achieves e4! We will take a central pawn with our f-pawn.} 10...fxe4 11.dxe4 {White recaptures.} 11...Qe8 {The queen has good play on the light squares. White has achieved their central pawn break, but on the other hand our rook is now well-placed on the half-open f-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: Beating The Reti / Study 1: The Reti with a Queenside Fianchetto / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/0b2870f6-73c4-467e-a424-25bf46685bc6/studies/2c58b574-7998-46f6-8809-5b79508abc25/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...b6 2.b3 {b3, with the idea of a fianchetto to control the a1-h8 diagonal. We fianchetto, stepping into the a8-h1 diagonal.} 2...Bb7 3.Bb2 {White controls their diagonal too. We play e6, getting ready for a potential d5 or f5 pawn push. Here White has many moves available to choose from. We'll go over the most popular ones. Don't move your bishop on f8 until you block off the white's bishop's access to g7!} 3...e6 4.c4 {Finally c4 is suggested by the engine, and it's played by some people. The idea is to fight for control of d5. We can develop and block the bishop's sight down the long diagonal.} 4...Nf6 5.g3 {If White goes for a double fianchetto... We can strike in the center with d5!} 5...d5 6.cxd5 {White may capture our center pawn. We will recapture with the e-pawn.} 6...exd5 7.Bg2 {White fianchettoes. Here we will bring our bishop out.} 7...Be7 8.O-O {White castles.} 8...O-O {And we castle, too. We have good control over the e4-square: the knight might end up there in the future. Simple moves like ...Nbd7 developing and ...c6 supporting the center are useful to us.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: Beating The Reti / Study 1: The Reti with a Queenside Fianchetto / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/0b2870f6-73c4-467e-a424-25bf46685bc6/studies/2c58b574-7998-46f6-8809-5b79508abc25/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...b6 2.b3 {b3, with the idea of a fianchetto to control the a1-h8 diagonal. We fianchetto, stepping into the a8-h1 diagonal.} 2...Bb7 3.Bb2 {White controls their diagonal too. We play e6, getting ready for a potential d5 or f5 pawn push. Here White has many moves available to choose from. We'll go over the most popular ones. Don't move your bishop on f8 until you block off the white's bishop's access to g7!} 3...e6 4.e3 {By far the most popular move in this position. It opens up the light-squared bishop. Let's go f5 and control e4!} 4...f5 5.Be2 {White continues to develop, preparing castling. Let's block off the bishop's view of g7.} 5...Nf6 6.O-O {White castles. Here we can go for the double fianchetto.} 6...g6 7.d4 {White gains space in the center. We fianchetto.} 7...Bg7 8.c4 {White proceeds with c4, fighting for control of d5.} 8...O-O {We simply castle. We have a nice grip over the e4 square. The main plan is to bring the queen to e7, push ...d6, develop the knight and later even go for a ...c5 push.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: Beating The Reti / Study 2: The Reti with a Kingside Fianchetto / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/0b2870f6-73c4-467e-a424-25bf46685bc6/studies/470f0688-1c45-4a01-b592-0079077403f3/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...b6 2.g3 {White intends to go on the same diagonal we're going. Copycat! We'll get there first!} 2...Bb7 3.Bg2 {White fianchettoes. We prepare to bring out our other bishop.} 3...e6 4.O-O {White castles their king into safety. This defends the bishop on g2. Beware of any discoveries by the knight! We will go f5, controlling e4. If White plays d4 or c4, we'll be in familiar territory.} 4...f5 5.d3 {White can choose to continue this way, preparing an e2-e4 push. We develop, fighting for control of e4.} 5...Nf6 6.e4 {White pushes e4. It is supported tactically, as we will see! Let's make use of our f-pawn by capturing a central pawn.} 6...fxe4 7.Ng5 {This pins and attacks our pawn, with the idea of recapturing on e4. Let's just develop. We can't stop White regaining their pawn.} 7...Nc6 8.Nxe4 {This move centralizes the knight. We will just develop a piece and get closer to castling.} 8...Be7 9.Nbc3 {White develops a knight, protecting its comrade on e4. We will castle into safety.} 9...O-O 10.Nxf6+ {White opts to exchange knights, figuring the c3-knight can replace the other knight by hopping to e4 itself. We will take back on f6 with the bishop.} 10...Bxf6 11.Ne4 {White brings the knight into the center, threatening to grab the bishop pair in an open position. We will preserve our bishop.} 11...Be7 12.Bd2 {White develops the bishop, probably with the idea of activating it on c3. We can expand and gain a tempo on the knight!} 12...d5 13.Nc3 {White is obliged to return to c3, where the bishop would have liked to go. We will place the queen on d7, defending our backwards e6 pawn and preparing to connect the rooks.} 13...Qd7 14.Qg4 {White develops the queen. This is a tricky move, threatening to capture on d5 thanks to the pin on the e-pawn.} 14...Ne5 {So let's allow our bishop to defend d5 as well, while gaining a tempo on the queen, with the idea of later settling our knight on g6!} *

[Event "Chapter 9: Beating The Reti / Study 2: The Reti with a Kingside Fianchetto / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/0b2870f6-73c4-467e-a424-25bf46685bc6/studies/470f0688-1c45-4a01-b592-0079077403f3/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...b6 2.g3 {White intends to go on the same diagonal we're going. Copycat! We'll get there first!} 2...Bb7 3.Bg2 {White fianchettoes. We prepare to bring out our other bishop.} 3...e6 4.O-O {White castles their king into safety. This defends the bishop on g2. Beware of any discoveries by the knight! We will go f5, controlling e4. If White plays d4 or c4, we'll be in familiar territory.} 4...f5 5.d3 {White can choose to continue this way, preparing an e2-e4 push. We develop, fighting for control of e4.} 5...Nf6 6.e4 {White pushes e4. It is supported tactically, as we will see! Let's make use of our f-pawn by capturing a central pawn.} 6...fxe4 7.Ng5 {This pins and attacks our pawn, with the idea of recapturing on e4. Let's just develop. We can't stop White regaining their pawn.} 7...Nc6 8.dxe4 {This move, on the other hand, seeks to maintain an e-pawn in the center. We can develop our bishop on d6, preventing an e5 push.} 8...Bd6 9.f4 {f4 might be played here, with the idea of going e5 and forking knight and bishop. We'll simply sidestep with check. Maybe White will later find that f4 has weakened their king position.} 9...Bc5+ 10.Kh1 {The king moves. We will push the g5-knight back. Perhaps White should have just centralized their knight by capturing on e4.} 10...h6 11.Nh3 {The knight retreats to h3. White needs to maintain the defense of the e4-pawn with the bishop.} 11...d6 {The move d6 applies some control to e5, and can be played as our bishop isn't behind the pawns anymore. Play will continue by securing the bishop on c5 via a7-a5, and castling.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: Beating The Reti / Study 2: The Reti with a Kingside Fianchetto / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/0b2870f6-73c4-467e-a424-25bf46685bc6/studies/470f0688-1c45-4a01-b592-0079077403f3/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...b6 2.g3 {White intends to go on the same diagonal we're going. Copycat! We'll get there first!} 2...Bb7 3.Bg2 {White fianchettoes. We prepare to bring out our other bishop.} 3...e6 4.O-O {White castles their king into safety. This defends the bishop on g2. Beware of any discoveries by the knight! We will go f5, controlling e4. If White plays d4 or c4, we'll be in familiar territory.} 4...f5 5.d3 {White can choose to continue this way, preparing an e2-e4 push. We develop, fighting for control of e4.} 5...Nf6 6.Nc3 {White may choose to prepare e4 with the knight, in which case... We get ready to trade our bishop for the knight and remove the supporter of e4.} 6...Bb4 7.Bd2 {White defends c3 to recapture with their bishop should we take. We will get our king into safety.} 7...O-O 8.e4 {White strikes with e4! Let's remove the knight supporting e4.} 8...Bxc3 9.Bxc3 {White recaptures with the bishop to preserve the integrity of their pawn structure. We take on e4, as this is better for us, structurally speaking.} 9...fxe4 10.Ng5 {The correct move for White. Our pawn is pinned to our bishop! With ...Nd5 we attack the bishop, but also we block off the long diagonal and revive capturing on d3 as a threat.} 10...Nd5 11.Qh5 {Checkmate its threatened! We need to deal with that before proceeding. We will attack the knight and block the checkmate threat. This forces White to move away with the knight.} 11...h6 12.Nxe4 {Qg6, threatening checkmate on h7 and g7 simultaneously, doesn't work, as we have Qxg5, removing the threat of mate on h7 and defending g7 with the queen. Therefore White recaptures the pawn, getting the knight out of danger. Let's eliminate the strong c3-bishop!} 12...Nxc3 13.bxc3 {White recaptures with the b-pawn. This threatens a Nf6+ discovery, which would win our b7-bishop and then the rook behind it on a8.} 13...Nc6 {Thus we here block the diagonal and protect against the discovery. We can reroute the knight to f5 and play ...Qe7 to connect the rooks!} *

[Event "Chapter 10: White's Other 1st Moves / Study 1: White Plays g3/b3/Nc3/e3 on Move 1 - Preparing against the 400s / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/cc0fa140-1d12-425c-ac66-a48827d46521/studies/dc4f68ae-131b-4d02-8779-d95cb35f3315/lines"]

1.g3 {Welcome to the final study of this course! Consider this a victory lap. Let's look at all the offbeat moves. White opens up with g3. Ok. This move means they'll fianchetto on the h1-a8 diagonal first. For that reason, we will take space in the center to try and punish White's passive start. We'll play ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Bg2 {White fianchettoes. Here we can continue by gaining space and central control.} 2...d5 3.d3 {White can go d3 in preparation of an e4 push. We'll develop to f6, fighting for control of the central squares.} 3...Nf6 4.Nf3 {White develops, placing pressure on our center. We'll develop, supporting it.} 4...Bd6 5.O-O {White castles. We will do likewise.} 5...O-O 6.Nbd2 {White prepares the thematic e4 push. We'll lend support to our center with c6.} 6...c6 7.e4 {White strikes in the center! But we can ignore the push and just develop our rook, defending e5.} 7...Re8 8.Re1 {White develops their rook, supporting e4. And we'll bring yet another piece out to overprotect e5.} 8...Nbd7 9.c3 {c3 is a popular move, White fights for control of d4 and maybe contemplates developing the queen on c2. We can begin a middle game plan of gaining space in the queenside. We're not really aiming to attack on the kingside any time soon, so queenside play it is.} 9...a5 10.Qc2 {White develops their queen. ...h6 is a useful move for us here, preventing a minor piece from ever landing on g5.} 10...h6 11.b3 {Giving the bishop scope to develop on b2. We can develop the queen, further supporting our pawn center.} 11...Qc7 12.Bb2 {A queenside fianchetto. We'll expand on the queenside.} 12...a4 13.bxa4 {If White takes it, temporarily gaining a pawn, we will exchange in the center.} 13...dxe4 14.dxe4 {White recaptures.} 14...Nc5 {And this knight jump places pressure on both e4 and a4, while reaching a square from which no pawn can remove it. Additionally, e6 will be a good square to develop the c8-bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: White's Other 1st Moves / Study 1: White Plays g3/b3/Nc3/e3 on Move 1 - Preparing against the 400s / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/cc0fa140-1d12-425c-ac66-a48827d46521/studies/dc4f68ae-131b-4d02-8779-d95cb35f3315/lines"]

1.g3 {Welcome to the final study of this course! Consider this a victory lap. Let's look at all the offbeat moves. White opens up with g3. Ok. This move means they'll fianchetto on the h1-a8 diagonal first. For that reason, we will take space in the center to try and punish White's passive start. We'll play ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Bg2 {White fianchettoes. Here we can continue by gaining space and central control.} 2...d5 3.c4 {Or White can play c4 right away, with the idea of undermining our center and giving their bishop greater scope. Here we can advance, gaining space.} 3...d4 4.d3 {White defends c4 and prevents our pawn from going further. We'll proceed with normal development.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White can develop pressurizing our center. We'll develop our bishop to c5 to make an e3 pawn break tougher to play.} 5...Bc5 6.O-O {White can castle. Now that our bishop is out we will develop the knight to e7.} 6...Nge7 7.a3 {White can play a3, with the idea of harassing our queenside pieces and gaining space.} 7...a5 {We can play a5 to counter this advance and to give our bishop retreating options. We'll be able to proceed with castling and getting our king to safety.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: White's Other 1st Moves / Study 1: White Plays g3/b3/Nc3/e3 on Move 1 - Preparing against the 400s / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/cc0fa140-1d12-425c-ac66-a48827d46521/studies/dc4f68ae-131b-4d02-8779-d95cb35f3315/lines"]

1.e3 {e3 is an unambitious first move. We can still go for our traditional setup.} 1...b6 2.d4 {White gains space in the center. We will fianchetto.} 2...Bb7 3.Nf3 {White develops, controlling e5. We'll open up lines for our other bishop by playing ...e6.} 3...e6 4.Be2 {White prepares to castle. We will go for our idea of e4 control.} 4...f5 5.O-O {White castles. And we will develop with an eye on e4.} 5...Nf6 6.c4 {After c4 gaining space for White... We will develop, getting ready to castle.} 6...Be7 7.Nc3 {White brings their knight out after pushing c4. And we can get our king to safety.} 7...O-O 8.b3 {White prepares to develop the bishop on b2.} 8...Ne4 {We jump our knight into the center. This is the end! Congratulations! Study the rest of the chapters, and keep this one in mind in case your opponents lash out with these rare options!} *

[Event "Chapter 10: White's Other 1st Moves / Study 1: White Plays g3/b3/Nc3/e3 on Move 1 - Preparing against the 400s / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/cc0fa140-1d12-425c-ac66-a48827d46521/studies/dc4f68ae-131b-4d02-8779-d95cb35f3315/lines"]

1.Nc3 1...b6 2.e4 {White usually will play a second move which merely transposes into our previous chapters. We fianchetto, as before.} 2...Bb7 3.d4 {White supports their two central pawns with pieces. We'll get ready to undermine one of the supporters of the pawn center.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {White goes for the Two Knights setup. We develop, attacking one of them.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {White supports their center.} 5...Nf6 {Meanwhile, we can continue piling up on e4 like in previous chapters.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: White's Other 1st Moves / Study 1: White Plays g3/b3/Nc3/e3 on Move 1 - Preparing against the 400s / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/b094bf9e-a914-4a41-a3b1-d0c921636166/chapters/cc0fa140-1d12-425c-ac66-a48827d46521/studies/dc4f68ae-131b-4d02-8779-d95cb35f3315/lines"]

1.b3 {Let's look at another offbeat move. White can start by fianchettoing their dark-squared bishop. This doesn't interrupt our plan, so we'll go back to our queenside fianchetto.} 1...b6 2.Bb2 {White fianchettoes. And so do we.} 2...Bb7 3.Nf3 {White develops a knight to control central squares before committing any pawns. We'll play as before.} 3...e6 4.d4 {White decides to play d4 and commit a pawn to the center. We continue with the plan we've previously demonstrated, and take control of the e4 square.} 4...f5 5.Nbd2 {White can continue with normal development. We'll continue to develop, controlling e4.} 5...Nf6 6.e3 {White supports d4 and gives their light-squared bishop scope to develop. The double fianchetto is our idea here.} 6...g6 7.Bd3 {White develops, attempting to address the issue of us having so much influence over the central e4 square. In reply we'll fianchetto.} 7...Bg7 8.O-O {White castles to get their king into safety.} 8...O-O {And we castle as well. As before, ...Qe7, ...d6, ...c5 — all of these moves are very natural in this position.} *